{"offset": 100, "next": 200, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0de90f8c4f431797dd9234e93d40c7ec85d3e285", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27714", "CorpusId": 293810067}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0de90f8c4f431797dd9234e93d40c7ec85d3e285", "title": "When Does Experience Memory Help Closed-Loop LLM Driving Agents? A Controlled Evaluation with Resampled Warmup Pools, Token-Matched Fillers, and Audited Baselines", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27714, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2516239785", "name": "Ying-Xin Jia"}, {"authorId": "2504354514", "name": "Xuan-Yi Li"}], "abstract": "When does experience memory help a closed-loop LLM driving agent? Published gains cannot say, because the two factors we find to matter most, which warmup experiences the pool contains and what any injected tokens do to the prompt, are controlled in none of them. We answer with a controlled evaluation on one audited HighwayEnv testbed (1Hz, gpt-4o-mini for the decisive contrasts). We treat the warmup pool as a resampled random factor (ten disjoint pools) and add token-matched irrelevant-filler controls, oracle-certified diagnostics, caution-pricing metrics, and quantified audits of the baseline ports. The probe suite spans flat-text retrieval (a DiLu port), a single-frame grid port (RESPOND-inspired), our episodic temporal-graph memory, a retrieval-free consolidated rulebook, and a flat K-step observation-history baseline. Four findings follow, each a property of this testbed. (1) Headline memory gains belong to the warmup draw. The reported pool is the best of ten for both retrieval memories, which beat No-Memory in only one pool of ten (sign test p=0.011), and even the rulebook's apparent pool-immunity collapsed on five fresh draws. (2) No retrieval design tested exceeds token-matched filler. The collision-only merge ceiling is reached by a single 15-token caution instruction, and metrics that price caution erase the rulebook's lead. (3) Representational sophistication is nowhere robustly operative. (4) Baseline port quality silently inverts conclusions. On this testbed, a single-pool comparison without filler controls would have reported memory gains that these controls reverse. We release the testbed, audited ports, seeds, and caches."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bc31aa213525efa8ae6c719200d5b0c497300a4f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28386", "CorpusId": 293811998}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bc31aa213525efa8ae6c719200d5b0c497300a4f", "title": "VisualEvolve: A Skill Self-Evolution Framework for Visual Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28386, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2516246733", "name": "Yu-Cun Li"}, {"authorId": "2516234771", "name": "Xue-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2516243744", "name": "Sheng-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2516055551", "name": "Xin-Quan Gong"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "2258285591", "name": "Yu-Quan Le"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2147412314", "name": "Da Cao"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Visual tool use enables vision-language models (VLMs) to acquire task-relevant evidence, but its effectiveness can be limited by predefined visual operations and the tool-use strategies learned during training. Representing these operations and strategies as reusable external skills allows them to be refined through self-evolution without updating model parameters. However, skill refinement can introduce performance regressions. Meanwhile, inspecting accumulated task execution trajectories together with skill evolution history may lead to evolution evidence overload. To address these challenges, we propose VisualEvolve, a self-evolving framework that evolves executable visual operations and tool-use strategies for VLMs through a Select--Execute--Evolve--Evaluate cycle. Skill-Library Snapshot Selection uses performance on a fixed validation set disjoint from execution tasks to select an effective historical snapshot for subsequent evolution, limiting the propagation of performance regressions. Progressive Evidence Access (PEA) guides capability-gap diagnosis through a coarse-to-fine hierarchy comprising the Skill-Performance Report, Evolution Log, Trajectory Summaries, and relevant raw trajectory segments. Across four VLM backbones and six evaluation sets from three benchmarks, VisualEvolve improves over the Initial Skill Library in 23 of 24 settings, with a maximum gain of 15.1 percentage points on VisualProbe Hard using Qwen3.5-Plus. Ablation studies support the contributions of Snapshot Selection and PEA, while further experiments show that skills can be improved using experience from different data settings and directly reused across backbones. Moreover, VisualEvolve enables effective skill self-evolution with over an order of magnitude fewer task-solving trajectories than estimated for representative RL-based methods."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ea5ad53bb05b9e41faf7fe19ea53d94b23a9d413", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28069", "CorpusId": 293812221}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ea5ad53bb05b9e41faf7fe19ea53d94b23a9d413", "title": "Training the Whole Agent, Not Just the Model", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28069, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2266119899", "name": "Ling-Xiao Tang"}, {"authorId": "2112528845", "name": "Wei Tao"}, {"authorId": "2318303849", "name": "Yi-Wen Guo"}, {"authorId": "2385724318", "name": "Ling-Feng Bao"}], "abstract": "Training coding agents typically updates the policy while keeping the harness fixed. During training, trajectories reveal flawed repair strategies and interaction problems that vary across tasks and change as the policy learns. We introduce HarnessAdapt-RL, which trains the policy and adapts its harness during reinforcement learning. For flawed repair strategies, a frozen teacher generates task-specific mechanism-level skills. For interaction problems recurring across tasks, a separate teacher model evolves tools and hooks, keeping each change only if it improves the observed pass rate or the observed trainable rate without lowering either. The policy trains with the adapted harness, and its new trajectories guide further adaptation. With a 9B base model, HarnessAdapt-RL improves pass@1 over GRPO by 9.8, 12.6, and 6.4 percentage points on SWE-bench Verified, Multilingual, and Pro, and outperforms GRPO with the human-designed OpenHands harness. Gains from mechanism-level skills persist after their removal at evaluation. Re-evolving tools and hooks as the policy changes outperforms freezing early versions. Removing them at deployment, in contrast, costs up to 7.7 percentage points. Training therefore produces a model-harness pair: the trained policy and the evolved tools and hooks, with skills removed at deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28914", "CorpusId": 293844720}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/35cb61680cb6be63f332956da8b9b66d17fe7981", "title": "D-Skill: Distilling Teacher Knowledge through Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28914, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379614165", "name": "Shuo Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2265517766", "name": "Da-Cheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2346365389", "name": "Joseph Gonzalez"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents excel at complex tasks, but high inference costs make transferring their capabilities to smaller models essential. Traditional knowledge distillation achieves this through parameter updates, requiring access to the student's weights and additional training compute while encoding the transferred knowledge implicitly in the parameters. We introduce D-Skill, a framework that treats the teacher as a black box and distills its behavioral advantage into an interpretable natural-language skill by analyzing paired rollouts and identifying behavioral divergences and agreements. The resulting lessons are consolidated offline, with any compatible skill-update backend, into a skill document that is provided to the student at inference time. D-Skill constructs the skill entirely offline from fixed trajectories, without labels, further environment interaction, or weight updates after rollout collection. Across SpreadsheetBench-Verified, ALFWorld, and chess puzzles spanning multiple teacher-student pairs, D-Skill closes 75%-95% of the teacher-student gap without touching model weights. After a single offline pass over the paired traces, D-Skill outperforms one-epoch SFT baselines and remains competitive with multi-epoch SFT. Under the same model family, the resulting student also incurs 5.7x-8.3x lower inference cost per task than the teacher. Our results demonstrate that natural-language skill distillation offers a performant, interpretable, and efficient alternative to weight-based capability transfer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "57b1b31d132f312efdda6f096bd507ee18971849", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28892", "CorpusId": 293844791}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/57b1b31d132f312efdda6f096bd507ee18971849", "title": "Combating Catastrophic Forgetting in Continual Domain Adaptation via Knowledge Recasting", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28892, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342746625", "name": "Zi-Jie Liu"}, {"authorId": "2354228808", "name": "Zhen Tan"}, {"authorId": "2394170606", "name": "Charles Fleming"}, {"authorId": "2353158386", "name": "Tian-Long Chen"}], "abstract": "Adapting language models to new domains during post-training risks degrading previously acquired capabilities, a phenomenon commonly known as catastrophic forgetting. Existing mitigation methods largely treat forgetting as a consequence of excessive model drift. They therefore constrain updates by preserving old outputs, replaying old data, or regularizing changes to parameters and representations so that the updated model remains close to its previous state. We take a different view: Rather than pulling the updated model back toward its old state, we translate the new domain into the model's pre-existing knowledge space, making unfamiliar knowledge learnable through familiar procedures. Therefore, we propose Skill Schema Transport (SST), a post-training framework that transports new knowledge into the model's existing skill space. SST abstracts each example type into a skill schema, preserving its essential operations, dependencies, and verification steps while removing domain-specific surface forms. By recasting new examples as instances of reusable procedures, SST turns knowledge update from trajectory memorization into procedural reuse. In continual domain adaptation settings, SST consistently reduces forgetting while improving generalization to related tasks. Across two 3B instruction-tuned backbones, SST improves average final performance on streamed domains by 2.53 to 5.75 points over sequential post-training. It also lowers max-drop forgetting in all evaluated settings, with the largest reduction from 42.41 to 7.10 points on the Qwen-3B MATH stream, while substantially recovering the OOD degradation induced by sequential updates."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4dc7ccb274f778815f8ccf0166f0044be0771851", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26453", "CorpusId": 293800713}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4dc7ccb274f778815f8ccf0166f0044be0771851", "title": "Learning What to Remember: Resource-Efficient Self-Improvement for Lifelong Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26453, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2339270147", "name": "Guannan Lai"}, {"authorId": "2515835290", "name": "Zi-Yang Bai"}, {"authorId": "2304704645", "name": "Jun-Peng Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2304399570", "name": "Han-Jia Ye"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agents aim to continuously improve their behavior by learning from experience after deployment. However, accumulating and reusing more experience does not necessarily lead to better decisions, particularly when generation, interaction, and memory resources are jointly constrained. Under such stringent constraints, we find that several existing lifelong-memory methods provide limited benefits over simple retrieval or even no-memory agents. This reveals a fundamental challenge for resource-efficient self-improvement: agents must learn not only which past experiences are relevant, but which are actually worth reusing to improve future decisions. When memory capacity and opportunities for reasoning and recovery are limited, retrieving an ineffective experience can consume scarce resources and hinder downstream execution. To address this challenge, we propose TokenWorth, a lightweight feedback-driven memory adaptation framework that learns query-dependent memory worth from sparse execution feedback. Without updating the backbone model, TokenWorth uses the learned worth to guide both experience retrieval and bounded memory management, enabling the agent to continually adapt how it reuses accumulated experience. Across three LifelongAgentBench environments and both local and API-based deployments, TokenWorth achieves the highest task success rate while using the fewest output tokens among the evaluated methods under the specified resource budgets. These results suggest that effective self-improvement under resource constraints depends not on accumulating more experience, but on learning what is worth remembering. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/AIGNLAI/TokenWorth."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e14d9852fb968ade7b06e88199ff27ec54aeec5a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25959", "CorpusId": 293802677}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e14d9852fb968ade7b06e88199ff27ec54aeec5a", "title": "EmbodiedWorld-200K: Toward Embodied World Agents via Embodied-Planning Dataset and Interactive World Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25959, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515984635", "name": "X. Feng"}, {"authorId": "2516004231", "name": "J. Tang"}, {"authorId": "2515978844", "name": "Z. Lin"}, {"authorId": "2265722184", "name": "L. Chen"}, {"authorId": "2362456241", "name": "S. Shao"}, {"authorId": "2516002638", "name": "Q. Lu"}, {"authorId": "2515985584", "name": "K. Huang"}], "abstract": "Building embodied agents that can perceive, reason, and act across diverse 3D worlds is a central research direction toward artificial general intelligence. Yet most existing work remains confined to specific tasks in a single scene and transfers poorly to new environments, leaving general embodied agents for open-world settings under-explored. We refer to such agents as Embodied World Agents (EWAs), whose defining property is the ability to understand diverse visual environments and execute general actions in open-world conditions. To advance EWA research, we study open-world embodied navigation-planning and release EmbodiedWorld-200K, a large-scale dataset of over 200K samples spanning diverse scenes. Each example is equipped with multi-level annotations, ranging from low-level camera-motion trajectories to high-level semantic instructions. The dataset also provides unified evaluation splits and task-specific metrics, supporting offline pretraining and fair evaluation of EWAs. Beyond offline supervision, a defining characteristic of an EWA is its interaction with the environment; training solely on offline data is therefore insufficient, especially for generalizing to scenes not covered in the training distribution. Building dedicated real interactive environments, however, is often prohibitively expensive. To address this, we propose a novel training scheme that employs a video world model as a low-cost environment proxy and performs interactive reinforcement-learning (RL) post-training of the EWA within it. Empirically, pretraining on EmbodiedWorld-200K substantially improves the open-world embodied planning ability of the EWA, and further RL post-training inside a world model yields clear gains on out-of-distribution scenes. The dataset, baseline checkpoints, and training and evaluation code are available at https://xiaokunfeng.github.io/EmbodiedWorld-200K/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "aa95c9da30f2cf0c9aff67f56a8cb00f02cc2d4e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25931", "CorpusId": 293800030}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/aa95c9da30f2cf0c9aff67f56a8cb00f02cc2d4e", "title": "EvoSkill v2: Fully Agentic Skill Evolution from Benchmark Adaptation to a Stateful Evolver", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25931, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505037074", "name": "Da-Wei Huang"}, {"authorId": "2408455839", "name": "Baran Nama"}, {"authorId": "2186553606", "name": "Salaheddin Alzubi"}], "abstract": "EvoSkill v2 is a three-agent system that edits the instructions (skills) an agent is given, leaving its model and harness intact. The user starts a run by describing a benchmark and supplying a grader, which a benchmark adaptor turns into a benchmark-specific plugin. A solver attempts the tasks, and an evolver reads the solver's training rollouts and rewrites the skills at each epoch. We implement the evolver as one stateful coding agent with a bash tool for the whole run, unlike the two most common designs among the skill optimizers we surveyed: a sequence of single model calls, as in SkillOpt, and an agent that starts afresh each round, as in SkillBoost. We also measure how much the solver's rollouts vary from one attempt to the next, give the evolver several attempts per training task, and accept every epoch's skills instead of selecting among them on noisy scores. With fine-grained ground truth, EvoSkill v2's skills raised the held-out pass rate above the stronger of SkillOpt and SkillBoost by 7.7 percentage points on SpreadsheetBench v2 and by 5.4 on $\\tau^3$-bench banking. In both benchmarks and both feedback settings, EvoSkill v2's evolver cost less per percentage point of held-out pass rate gained than either baseline's optimizer. EvoSkill v2 also enables a less capable model to improve its own skills, demonstrated by a gain of up to 21.5 percentage points in the $\\tau^3$-bench banking experiment where Claude Haiku 5.5 powers both solver and evolver."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5b3933cddde122f8828cceef16bd04527d28dfec", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25985", "CorpusId": 293800915}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5b3933cddde122f8828cceef16bd04527d28dfec", "title": "NIOCIS: Typed-Decision Next-Hop Routing with Node-Conditioned Option Spaces for Tool-Use Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25985, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2524814915", "name": "Jin-Sung Han"}, {"authorId": "2037488623", "name": "Ming-Feng Xin"}, {"authorId": "2399164305", "name": "Feng Liang"}, {"authorId": "2515997878", "name": "J. Yu"}], "abstract": "Tool-use agents pay per decision: at each hop a language model re-reads the state and thinks for seconds, though its actions are finite and decisions recur. We present NIOCIS, a routing layer that folds execution logs into a loop-collapsed action graph (forward transitions plus recovery back-edges) answering one typed question: which declared option at a state, plus stop, does the fleet take next? A small model reads a calibrated distribution over them in one pass, emitting no text. On 1,615 production security-operations episodes we measure what the layer replaces, returns and is bounded by. Replaces: the typed encoder matches the logged next hop on 32.2% of the 3,875 held-out decisions (34.9% within the declared options) in 10 ms of encoder time (19.5 ms for the typed-choice form), against 2.2 s per LLM hop; two feature readouts reach 32.8% and 32.3%, an ordering the paired test does not resolve, so the typed layer is chosen for cost, not a separated lead. Tuning: jpt-4b reaches 9.9% zero-shot and 31.9% fine-tuned, a gap an exact paired test does not resolve (p=0.68), at 244 ms. Returns little: the realised hop falls outside the declared options for 7.9% of decisions and outside the model's top twenty for 9.3%; the most confident 15% holds 91.9% accuracy. Bounded: a cross-trace leave-one-out test puts the released state's ceiling at 25.6--44.9% as the state key grows more specific, i.e. repeated states disagree more often than they agree; the option-space ablation attributes the gain to per-node candidate restriction (+3.5 points over a flat catalog) rather than graph traversal (zero within its interval); a gated self-evolution loop lifts every arm above its anchor yet ends below full supervision; a confidence-gated cascade loses agreement when the escalation arm is the zero-shot stand-in; and agreement past a third of the fleet needs a state carrying the plan and the observation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "664395f966d458896bc6ecf2e279689e48cee88b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26848", "CorpusId": 293804891}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/664395f966d458896bc6ecf2e279689e48cee88b", "title": "Where is Physical RSI capability from? An Observational Scaling Law of LLM-as-policy", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26848, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2457303785", "name": "Ze-Kai Li"}, {"authorId": "2515999943", "name": "Jia-Yi Yu"}, {"authorId": "2506270318", "name": "Jin-Lu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382919712", "name": "Lu-Yang Niu"}, {"authorId": "2385150703", "name": "Ze-Kun Qi"}, {"authorId": "2501918775", "name": "Jun-Yang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2265920777", "name": "Xu-Chuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2301193049", "name": "Dai-En Liu"}, {"authorId": "2440685250", "name": "Yintianrun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2336953381", "name": "He Wang"}, {"authorId": "2242612318", "name": "Li Yi"}], "abstract": "In this paper, we systematically investigate the mechanisms, scaling behavior, and generalization of physical Recursive self-improvement. We introduce PhysRSI-eval to quantify physical RSI through a five-axis metric: First-Attempt Performance, Efficiency, Success, Budget and Improvement from failure. Different VLMs are evaluated on 18 manipulation tasks under a fixed interaction budget. The analysis of experimental results motivates the Physical Barrel Hypothesis: different capabilities may constrain different parts of improvement. The five-axis metrics also distinguish systems with close public benchmark averages and show that high scores need not come with early or efficient gains. We study observational scaling from two perspectives: predicting the relative performance of VLM agents from their public capabilities, and forecasting later progress from early physical feedback. Together, these analyses ask how quickly and how far an agent can improve. Moreover, we conduct workspace interventions and then analyze what improvement leaves behind. Removing history reduces late-round success, frozen workspaces remain useful on unseen layouts, and three cross-task cases achieve success earlier. The results suggest that scene-specific adaptation and reusable experience can coexist."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "700a5af40b25a294dae6381d71a7c41d120faa4e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26098", "CorpusId": 293807466}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/700a5af40b25a294dae6381d71a7c41d120faa4e", "title": "From Code to Playable Systems: CEGame-Bench for Long-Horizon Game Creation Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26098, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2304549951", "name": "Zi-Xuan Dong"}, {"authorId": "2377579105", "name": "Yang-Hai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2315540800", "name": "Han-Yan Bian"}, {"authorId": "2503712639", "name": "Ji Tao"}, {"authorId": "2299106618", "name": "Jun-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2515993517", "name": "Jia-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2408464177", "name": "Xin-Yu Che"}, {"authorId": "2515997552", "name": "Lin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2304423465", "name": "Bao-Yun Peng"}, {"authorId": "2363498987", "name": "Xiao-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2346051109", "name": "Jia-Heng Liu"}], "abstract": "A game ships when a player can launch it, finish it, and return to a saved world, none of which a successful build guarantees, yet game-creation agents are mostly tested on one engine or on one-prompt browser games. We present CEGame-Bench, the first long-horizon benchmark for complete-game creation across five production engines: Defold, Godot, Roblox Studio, Unity, and Unreal Engine. Agents build each game from an empty project and a design document in runs averaging up to 295 steps and 65M tokens; the document doubles as a contract, and a hidden evaluator accepts a game only by real-input play. Under four scaffolds, agents earn most integration points but usually under half the gameplay points, and their games reach the ending in only 16% of runs, while references built by engineers with coding agents score near the maximum, which places this artifact-to-play gap in the agents. No two engines rank configurations alike (Kendall's tau 0.14-0.62), and the same game usually scores higher on Unity and Unreal Engine, which check more before play. We release Game Completion Boost, an agent skill for requirement-driven playtesting and repair that raises the execution-based score of three models by 14.1-24.3 points."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7b75807f8aac9b83b41d5df0216fd2fafb376690", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27406", "CorpusId": 293809106}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7b75807f8aac9b83b41d5df0216fd2fafb376690", "title": "More Memory, Worse Agents: Error Reproduction and Anti-Persistence in LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27406, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328617416", "name": "Ru-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "1518267866", "name": "Kishan Panaganti"}, {"authorId": "2292161506", "name": "Dong-Ruo Zhou"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can improve across tasks without updating model parameters by storing experience from their own executions in external memory and reusing it to guide later decisions. A central design question is whether to retain only observed trajectories and outcomes or also the rules, summaries, and skills inferred from them. Retaining these abstractions allows agents to reuse strategies without repeatedly deriving them from raw trajectories. However, errors in stored abstractions can propagate to subsequent decisions through retrieval and reuse. They can also shape execution trajectories, which then become the basis for new abstractions that reproduce the original errors. We term this feedback mechanism error reproduction: errors are regenerated through a cycle of abstraction, execution, and re-abstraction, extending their persistence beyond direct reuse of the original faulty knowledge. We propose Anti-Persistence, a memory design that accumulates trajectories and outcomes while reconstructing task-specific guidance on demand. An adaptive selector uses prior outcomes to decide whether and how to synthesize guidance, which is discarded after execution. This prevents its direct reuse as a stored object, although indirect feedback through retained trajectories remains possible. Across different benchmarks and backbones, Anti-Persistence achieves higher overall held-out success and the lowest average LLM-call cost among the evaluated memory designs. These results support separating experience accumulation from abstraction persistence in memorybased self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d5f66db7accd06cdb3bd07f3935257285e1ee2a3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27227", "CorpusId": 293809978}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d5f66db7accd06cdb3bd07f3935257285e1ee2a3", "title": "Which Agent's Skill Should Change? Group-Relative Credit Assignment for Evolving Multi-Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27227, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291054660", "name": "Ya-Nan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2470772938", "name": "Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2386515532", "name": "David Le"}, {"authorId": "2316523439", "name": "Chen-Han Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2328724165", "name": "Yi-Zheng Sun"}, {"authorId": "2379686539", "name": "Guang-Wei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2366694043", "name": "Cheng-Hao Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2279451646", "name": "Cheng-Hua Lin"}, {"authorId": "2505632305", "name": "Imon Banerjee"}], "abstract": "Cooperative language-model agents can improve without weight updates by revising reusable skills, but only if a shared team reward is traced to the responsible agent and skill. Existing methods credit the skills present in each trajectory, yet presence is not responsibility: a harmful skill active in successful runs still earns positive credit. We present \\textbf{GRSE}, \\textbf{G}roup-\\textbf{R}elative \\textbf{S}kill \\textbf{E}volution, which assigns behavioral credit by comparison: inspired by GRPO, it executes each question several times under the same skill libraries and contrasts the executions to credit agent--skill pairs, with structured diagnosis of failures. On HotpotQA and LoCoMo with two open-weight actors, GRSE outperforms MASkills, the closest prior method, on HotpotQA and every LoCoMo category except open-domain, with the largest gains on multi-hop questions ($+6.7$ and $+3.2$ F1). Its gains rest on better-grounded retrieval rather than answers given without evidence, and its learned skills transfer between the multi-hop tasks. GRSE is robust to corrupted libraries: starting from planted harmful skills, it credits every planted skill with the correct sign and recovers the score of a clean library ($+6.3$ points), whereas MASkills misattributes two and falls below its starting point."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8dc5714e012d22c33055df5d1172618a49d48860", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27327", "CorpusId": 293809358}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8dc5714e012d22c33055df5d1172618a49d48860", "title": "Unsupervised Tool Evolution for Coding Agents in Data-Lake Analytics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27327, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2145435513", "name": "Yuqing Yang"}, {"authorId": "2262420822", "name": "Yurong Liu"}, {"authorId": "2135593484", "name": "Deqing Fu"}, {"authorId": "2055805642", "name": "Manav Garg"}, {"authorId": "40696276", "name": "S. Bodapati"}, {"authorId": "2324057763", "name": "Fatma Ozcan"}, {"authorId": "1770962", "name": "A. Halevy"}, {"authorId": "2258747730", "name": "Jia-Ni Zhang"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can answer analytical questions over a data lake by discovering, cleaning, and joining the relevant data. However, they discard this work after each question and redo it from scratch for the next one. Tool evolution, which automatically builds and refines reusable tools across tasks, is a natural fit for this repetition. Since a ground-truth label for a data-lake question requires an expert to solve it first, we consider the unsupervised setting. We propose TEAL (Tool Evolution for Analytics over data Lakes), which evolves a library of tools and usage skills for a given Worker agent without labels. Two agents propose tools from complementary evidence, an Explorer from the data lake itself and a Critic from Worker trajectories. A proposed change is kept only if the Worker adopts the tool on a rerun without extra steps and any changed answer is supported by trajectory evidence. We also introduce TEAL-Q, which generates synthetic questions for tool evolution when real ones are unavailable. We evaluate on KramaBench and LakeQA with four Worker LLMs under step budgets, which cap the number of steps the Worker may take per question. With a tight budget of 10 or 15 steps, TEAL improves every Worker on both benchmarks, lifting Sonnet-5 at 15 steps by 9.3 points on KramaBench and 21.7 points on LakeQA. A tighter budget also yields better feedback for evolution. For example, Sonnet-5's LakeQA library evolved at 20 steps and deployed unchanged at 30 steps scores 68.3, above both the baseline (59.6) and a library evolved directly at 30 steps (64.2). Additionally, a library evolved on synthetic questions performs comparably to one evolved on real questions. These results show that useful tools can be evolved without labels, and hopefully inspire future work on what to evolve and how to obtain feedback in unsupervised agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d0d8083d90a4990d37ff04ef95ba160907412019", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27377", "CorpusId": 293809761}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0d8083d90a4990d37ff04ef95ba160907412019", "title": "REM: What to Store, When to Recall, What to Forget in Experience Memory for Coding Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27377, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510546322", "name": "Xin-You Chen"}, {"authorId": "2201067308", "name": "Pedro Herrero-Vidal"}, {"authorId": "2499363552", "name": "Xiao-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2276527790", "name": "Xiao-Jing Yu"}, {"authorId": "2324679735", "name": "Cris Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266173370", "name": "Bing-Qing Song"}, {"authorId": "2516258861", "name": "Bin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2265719701", "name": "Suleiman A. Khan"}, {"authorId": "2294390078", "name": "You Chen"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents now autonomously complete long-horizon tasks, such as repository-level coding, through hundreds of think-act steps, yet the experience each run produces is rarely reused on the next. Prior memory systems concentrate on retrieval, finding the most relevant past trajectory. Retrieval cannot observe the running agent, so it cannot decide whether the agent needs an experience, at which point, or for how long. We introduce REM (Reusable Experience Memory), a framework that manages the memory lifecycle from inside the agent's think-act loop through three explicit decisions. What to store: each completed trajectory is consolidated into a phase-structured document, two orders of magnitude smaller than the trajectory. When to recall: the document is injected at the start of the task; the costlier implementation record is withheld until an in-loop gate detects the agent struggling. What to forget: the record is evicted after a single exposure. On repository-level bug fixing (SWE-ContextBench Lite), REM retrieves the related trajectory more accurately than every state-of-the-art memory system (68.7% vs. 55.6% match@1) and improves, in every replicate, end-task resolution (27.6% vs. 24.9%) at 17% lower cost. Injecting the same experience as a summary or raw trajectory, even when oracle-retrieved, does not. Ablating any of the three decisions reduces resolution. Altogether, these results demonstrate that the bottleneck in experience reuse is not retrieval fidelity but how experience is stored and governed across its lifecycle, opening a control-theoretic perspective on agent memory."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bdc6c6174d06daf87116aed0e208e9760e2cf6d3", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1002/gamm.70016", "CorpusId": 293827370}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bdc6c6174d06daf87116aed0e208e9760e2cf6d3", "title": "Agentic artificial intelligence in mechanics of materials: A review of building tailored models on demand", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/gamm.70016", "status": "HYBRID", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1002/gamm.70016?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1002/gamm.70016, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2332536882", "name": "M. Tacke"}, {"authorId": "2332536211", "name": "Matthias Busch"}, {"authorId": "2364310856", "name": "Kevin Linka"}, {"authorId": "2364117705", "name": "Roland C. Aydin"}, {"authorId": "2391259767", "name": "Christian J. Cyron"}], "abstract": "\n \n In mechanics of materials, many powerful methods are accessible only to experts. Constitutive models, for example, describe the relation between stress and strain in materials and are a core ingredient of simulations from structural engineering to biomechanics. Yet building and using such models requires specialized expertise\u2014a barrier for the many potential users trained in other fields. Artificial intelligence based on foundation models, increasingly organized as agents, is beginning to lower this barrier by allowing users to generate tailored models on demand. The same development is underway in many other fields where specialized tools have long been hand\u2010crafted anew for each task. We call this emerging paradigm the\n on\u2010the\u2010fly generation of physical models\n : from a plain request, an agent creates a physical model on demand\u2014from scratch and tailored to the problem. Its appeal is accessibility and flexibility. As a consequence, the lasting artifact is no longer a fixed model but the orchestration and prompting of the agents that produce it. This review surveys the concepts behind the paradigm, within mechanics of materials and in neighboring fields, and examines them through two lenses: effectiveness (the accuracy of the generated models) and trustworthiness (their physical admissibility). Trustworthiness is pursued along two routes: deepening the agent's integration under explicit validation, or restraining its autonomy in favor of trusted components. Given the rapid progress of foundation models, and the bitter lesson that general methods leveraging computation tend to prevail, we call for paradigms designed to scale with this progress rather than to compete with it.\n"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "010cde7453048f2bbaa30eeb2fb3029661968536", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001", "CorpusId": 293865503}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/010cde7453048f2bbaa30eeb2fb3029661968536", "title": "AI Welfare", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": "CLOSED", "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1093/9780197840375.001.0001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2286079241", "name": "Simon Goldstein"}, {"authorId": "1423334939", "name": "Cameron Domenico Kirk-Giannini"}], "abstract": "\n This book systematically investigates the possibility of artificial intelligence (AI) welfare. It focuses on three plausible sufficient conditions for welfare: having beliefs and desires, being conscious, and feeling pleasure and displeasure. The book explores the leading philosophical theories of each condition and applies them to AIs. It argues that some existing AIs plausibly have beliefs and desires; that some existing AIs could plausibly be modified in small ways to become conscious; and that if these systems could be made conscious, they could easily be made to feel pleasure and displeasure. This constitutes a provisional case that AIs already or soon may have welfare. In addition, the book lays out nine open questions about AI welfare, as follows: How should one identify which desires contribute to welfare? Is consciousness required for welfare? Do simple AIs like Roombas have welfare? Do tiny neural networks satisfy the conditions of functionalist theories of consciousness? Does consciousness require rich representational formats? Could AIs experience bodily pleasures and displeasures? Are pleasure and displeasure essentially access conscious? Are simulated minds real? Are LLMs and related systems merely stochastic parrots? The book lays out provisional answers to each of these questions, while also leaving room for future discussion."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "471b5fd6bf1dc0c9836d70cbc84ebf31ff7b0a8c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25455", "CorpusId": 293805701}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/471b5fd6bf1dc0c9836d70cbc84ebf31ff7b0a8c", "title": "Self-Crystallizing Behavior Trees: Amortizing Foundation-Model Reasoning into Deterministic Control for Autonomous Drones", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25455, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "88969241", "name": "Behnam Moradi"}], "abstract": "Classical robot control, Finite State Machines and Behavior Trees (BTs), is deterministic and inspectable but fixed at design time: it fails on situations its designers did not anticipate. Foundation models can synthesize new control logic at runtime, at a cost in latency and non-determinism hard to accept in safety-critical aerial systems. Existing LLM-driven approaches keep the model in the loop permanently and discard what it produces. We propose Self-Crystallizing Behavior Trees (SCBT), in which the foundation model is a compiler rather than a runtime. When a BT node fails, an LLM proposes a minimal, mission-agnostic subtree patch; CMA-ES tunes its parameters in simulation; a contextual bandit decides whether a stored patch or a new LLM call is worth its cost; and a three-stage admission pipeline (static check, invariant-monitored replays, an empirical margin over the unpatched tree) admits only patches that demonstrably recover. Admitted patches are stored in a fleet library and, once the LLM call rate decays, crystallized into a deterministic BT that no human wrote. We implement SCBT on Microsoft Project AirSim with a schema-constrained LLM pipeline (Gemini 3.1 Pro) and a lockstep simulator. Over three seeds of a 20-episode learning phase with wind-gust and battery failures, SCBT used $11\\pm1.4$ LLM calls against 26 for whole-tree regeneration and 22 without a library, with 0.33 calls per episode in the second half. The crystallized trees, 28 and 33 nodes with two patches each, reached $97\\pm2\\%$ mission success with no learning running, against 12\\% for the unpatched tree and 98\\% for a hand-coded expert tree, and reproduce decision traces exactly when re-ticked offline. Two negative results: replay admission rejected useful wind recoveries by attributing wind-induced geofence excursions to the patch, and a static-only ablation generalized better to held-out failures."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a0086d13e5ec9ae5f8ad0564e9dff6b8b97256bd", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25374", "CorpusId": 293805216}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a0086d13e5ec9ae5f8ad0564e9dff6b8b97256bd", "title": "Mining the Self-Rewriting System: A Vision for Data Mining on AI-Native Infrastructure", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25374, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2439954", "name": "Mayank Darbari"}, {"authorId": "51219074", "name": "Chiman Salavati"}, {"authorId": "2264480350", "name": "Liang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2515994405", "name": "Da-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2515992458", "name": "Jiong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2319117545", "name": "Kelly Wan"}, {"authorId": "2099634416", "name": "Krishna Chakka"}, {"authorId": "2264620213", "name": "Liang-Jie Hong"}], "abstract": "Classical data mining treats data as generated outside the learner. Emerging AI-native infrastructure invalidates this assumption: as agents observe system state, act, update retrieval stores and models, and promote new artifacts into the serving path, their own decisions reshape the future logs, traces, corpora, and evaluation records used for later training and release. We call this new research object a self-rewriting system and the resulting system-induced distribution shift endogenous drift. Our central, falsifiable claim is that cross-surface causes are not identifiable in general from marginal per-surface monitoring: different coupled failures can produce the same individual signals while differing in their joint behavior, requiring joint analysis for reliable attribution. This agenda comprises four foundational research problems: fusing heterogeneous drift signals into calibrated gating and attribution (C1); mining agent traces whose activity alphabet evolves (C2); maintaining knowledge fabrics through online reindexing, faithfulness, and compression decisions (C3); and safely promoting concurrent learning streams through one shared evaluation gate (C4). These remain fragmented across concept drift, performative prediction, process mining, streaming retrieval-augmented generation, and MLOps, without a unified data-mining agenda. We outline public benchmarks and evaluation criteria, using AI-native telecom only as a motivating exemplar with analogues in finance, software CI/CD, and clinical decision support."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5cfacf02e804d95459ca0f7c4188948cfcef329c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25211", "CorpusId": 293807440}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5cfacf02e804d95459ca0f7c4188948cfcef329c", "title": "NeuroAct: Advancing EEG Analysis and Decoding via Dual-Loop Autonomous Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25211, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science", "Engineering"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2360371481", "name": "Wei Xiong"}, {"authorId": "2243033869", "name": "Jiang-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2188204871", "name": "Han Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2258307351", "name": "Lin-Xiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2360353275", "name": "Jie Li"}, {"authorId": "2249726937", "name": "Chang-Jun Jiang"}], "abstract": "EEG analysis and decoding underpin brain-computer interfaces, cognitive assessment, and sleep monitoring. Fixed pipelines and reward-driven search do not explicitly determine how signal structure, representations, predictor behavior, and training dynamics contribute to performance or generalization gaps, leaving intervention choice underdetermined. We introduce NeuroAct, an autonomous research agent framework for EEG analysis and decoding. NeuroAct implements dual-loop autonomous research across EEG foundation models, task-specific predictors, and model combinations. In an inner loop, research, development, and review roles use evidence on signal structure, representations, predictor behavior, and training dynamics to test hypotheses, select analyses or interventions, and refine candidates using performance, stability, and cost feedback. An outer loop distills outcomes into conditional, testable recommendations with task and model conditions, then validates and revises them on subsequent tasks.We evaluate four settings: (A) EEG General Analysis, (B) Cross-task Model Optimization, (C) Single-task Open Research, and (D) Collaborative Prediction. In Setting A, NeuroAct's 80.88 macro-average exceeds BrainAgent's 63.13 by 17.75 points. In Setting B, NeuroAct raises EEGPT's mean balanced accuracy (BA) by 7.34 points and HBN Pearson's (r) from 0.6894 to 0.7362; in Setting C, it improves ADFTD BA by 16.33 points; and in Setting D, it raises Workload BA from 79.69% to 85.02%. Experience reuse raises BCIC-IV-2a BA from 34.66% to 50.81% while reducing LLM tokens by 64.02%, indicating recommendations improve cross-task performance. NeuroAct enables evidence-guided EEG research improved by cross-task experience."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "856719103b0e2b77e483f4ca2a031250453625f5", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25078", "CorpusId": 293807336}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/856719103b0e2b77e483f4ca2a031250453625f5", "title": "CoEvo-RSI: When Harnesses Evolve, Which Models Should Follow?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25078, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2354046953", "name": "Jia-Qi Su"}, {"authorId": "2340212538", "name": "Gui-Min Zhang"}, {"authorId": "50878225", "name": "Jiejing Shao"}, {"authorId": "2309546812", "name": "Cong Pang"}, {"authorId": "2410033596", "name": "Tian-Kuo Yao"}, {"authorId": "1807998", "name": "I. Tsang"}, {"authorId": "2327301640", "name": "Hai-Yan Yin"}, {"authorId": "2367752546", "name": "Shui-Cheng Yan"}], "abstract": "Model--harness co-evolution has emerged as a prominent paradigm for recursive self-improving (RSI) agents, jointly evolving their execution scaffolds and intrinsic capabilities. We study the persistent yet often overlooked brain--body mismatch problem in this paradigm, where an evolving harness (body) demands behaviors its model (brain) has not learned or restricts capabilities it already possesses. We introduce CoEvo-RSI, a RSI framework that advances model--harness co-evolution through agent-driven, bidirectional historical inheritance. Drawing on a shared evolutionary archive and execution feedback, a meta-agent coordinates harness development from historical designs with model checkpoint selection or merging and subsequent adaptation under the selected harness, aligning inherited capabilities with evolving execution requirements to reduce brain--body mismatch. Using only supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on its own rejection-sampled trajectories, CoEvo-RSI makes Qwen3.5-9B competitive with larger models across diverse vertical applications, including multi-agent failure attribution, role-aware text-to-SQL, and smart-home instruction following. It surpasses DeepSeek-V4-Pro on TraceElephant (+14.16 agent accuracy) and GLM-5.3 on HomeBench VM (+4.90 success rate), underscoring the need for brain--body alignment and the potential of bidirectional historical inheritance to advance agent co-evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "61ad5be9e012580fdd14b77189a0fc5d4b1473c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24464", "CorpusId": 293687516}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/61ad5be9e012580fdd14b77189a0fc5d4b1473c7", "title": "Learning to Compose: Recursive Self-Improvement of Shared Executable Stacks for Zero-Shot Long-Horizon Robot Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24464, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2453994527", "name": "Yang-Zhi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2363891692", "name": "Fei-Ran Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505800464", "name": "Zi-Shuo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2278326150", "name": "Zi-Jian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2158762689", "name": "Xin Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2221288461", "name": "Lecheng Ruan"}, {"authorId": "2510511480", "name": "Wen-Juan Han"}], "abstract": "Individually competent robot skills can fail when composed: tools may lack a required capability, completion signals may misrepresent physical progress, and one skill may leave a state that violates its successor's prerequisites. We introduce Recursia, a recursive self-improvement framework that treats the shared executable stack, rather than individual task programs, as the persistent unit of improvement. The stack integrates tools, skills with execution contracts, handoffs, and verifiers. Source-side execution traces guide failure attribution and the generation of missing tools or repairs to existing shared components. Generality checks, mechanism tests, and paired selection and regression gates determine which changes become the next parent. The resulting stack is frozen and reused through declarative task adapters, allowing source-derived changes to support new compositions without target-side code repair. Experiments cover LIBERO-Pro Long simulation and a physical Franka FR3 equipped with a Franka Hand. In simulation, Recursia improves success over ASPIRE by 5.8 percentage points under Pos perturbations and 15.1 points under Task perturbations. Across three physical compositional target tasks, it improves mean success over the Skill Document Only baseline by 40 percentage points. Analyses of constituent execution, candidate rejection, and inference drivers distinguish local mechanism improvements from complete task success and characterize the cost of feedback-conditioned orchestration. These results examine shared executable interfaces as a concrete mechanism for compositional transfer in robot programming."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "341311ce43a43ec8016e3b0110c04607771b9e00", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24630", "CorpusId": 293686130}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/341311ce43a43ec8016e3b0110c04607771b9e00", "title": "Selm: Self-Evolving Library Management for Multi-Skill Agent Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24630, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515542932", "name": "Meng-Xi Hu"}, {"authorId": "2236902930", "name": "Yuxuan Hu"}, {"authorId": "2335609031", "name": "Jinbo Su"}, {"authorId": "2446823653", "name": "Ke Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330163563", "name": "Hong-Hao Shi"}, {"authorId": "2506645814", "name": "Wen-Li Wu"}, {"authorId": "2287979930", "name": "Cui-Ping Li"}, {"authorId": "2191043357", "name": "Hong Chen"}, {"authorId": "2404854579", "name": "Jing Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly use skills as a lightweight way to acquire task-specific knowledge. Recent work enables skills to evolve from task trajectories. However, these methods often lack systematic management of evolved skills, making it difficult for agents to reuse and accumulate skills across domains, thus limiting performance on subsequent tasks.To reliably accumulate, reuse, and maintain useful skills across tasks, we present SELM (Self-Evolving Library Management), a framework that formulates skill evolution as library-level management. SELM jointly manages skill retrieval, updating, and lifecycle by maintaining skill confidence and utility signals. It introduces utility-aware retrieval to select useful skills, a Map-Reduce framework to generate targeted updates from task-specific experience, and lifecycle controls to retain or remove skills based on their effectiveness. These mechanisms enable the skill library to continuously accumulate useful experience while preserving and pruning skills across tasks, improving agent performance on complex tasks across diverse domains. We evaluate SELM on four agent benchmarks covering diverse tasks and execution environments, and SELM consistently outperforms in most settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "80f63c0d884847c4d27502cee2cf79e156556968", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24452", "CorpusId": 293687733}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/80f63c0d884847c4d27502cee2cf79e156556968", "title": "HumanSkill-4D: Training-Free Language Querying of Animatable Gaussian Avatars in Space and Time", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24452, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2274204492", "name": "Junsu Kim"}, {"authorId": "8794013", "name": "N. Kim"}, {"authorId": "2086576", "name": "Dongyoon Han"}, {"authorId": "2274106948", "name": "Seungryul Baek"}], "abstract": "We introduce avatar referring segmentation, which selects regions of animatable Gaussian avatars from language queries. A query can also specify a pose or motion to select matching intervals. This lets users interact with avatars for local editing and motion inspection. However, making these selections with existing language Gaussian splatting methods poses two challenges: adjacent parts and garments are hard to separate and may be referred to indirectly (region ambiguity), while fine-grained motions can look alike in RGB (motion ambiguity). We address these challenges with HumanSkill-4D, a training-free framework that shares a multimodal large language model (MLLM) and geometric operators across avatars. For each avatar, a semantic memory links body-part and garment categories to persistent Gaussian regions and appearance descriptions. The MLLM uses category names and descriptions to select categories by appearance, relation, or function. To find matching intervals, it composes operators into programs that check query conditions on driving joint trajectories stored in a motion memory. These category choices and operator outputs make selections traceable, while composing or adding operators supports new motion conditions. To evaluate this task, we introduce three human-annotated benchmarks and show that HumanSkill-4D outperforms the baselines in part segmentation and 3D and 4D referring segmentation, with compact memories and only minutes of setup per avatar."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0547d476e4b763bb1f106ab0059614d9b854ac16", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24723", "CorpusId": 293805100}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0547d476e4b763bb1f106ab0059614d9b854ac16", "title": "Where Natural Language Ends in Agents: Executable Skill Composition with MetaSkill", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24723, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510370960", "name": "Meng-Xue Shu"}, {"authorId": "2243894203", "name": "Wei He"}, {"authorId": "2441642890", "name": "Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2449714955", "name": "Run-Ke Liu"}, {"authorId": "2364198997", "name": "Kai Han"}, {"authorId": "2399111239", "name": "Yun-He Wang"}], "abstract": "Agent Skills express open-ended task knowledge in natural language, but composing them requires persistent execution order and upstream-output handoffs. We propose selective formalization at Skill boundaries: coordination relations with stable references and machine-checkable constraints become executable, while substantive task judgments remain within Skills. We introduce MetaSkill, a hybrid representation that preserves complete Skill packages and local instructions while making invocation dependencies and predecessor-text bindings executable through a directed acyclic graph (DAG) runtime. This interface supports explicit coordination without prescribing how each Skill performs its local task. Across 20 multi-Skill artifact tasks and two LLMs, we compare natural-language procedures, prompt-visible graphs, and MetaSkill derived from a common frozen plan per task, using the same designated Skill packages. Additional comparisons evaluate AgentSkillOS with the same available Skill packages as MetaSkill, as well as agent systems using their configured tools. Blinded artifact judgments yield favorable preference point estimates across all reported comparisons, with 95% task-bootstrap intervals excluding parity against Prompt Graph under DeepSeek-V4-Flash and OpenClaw under GLM-5.2. In audited GLM-5.2 execution traces, all planned nodes complete and all declared precedence constraints are satisfied. Together, these findings support MetaSkill as a practical interface for composing open-ended Skills, combining local linguistic flexibility with executable dependencies, explicit predecessor-text bindings, and auditable coordination. Code is available at: https://github.com/TokenRhythm/opensquilla/tree/paper/metaskill-lp4fm-2026"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a3e524fa658b69f591ac9dd7720b51a4127a6063", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22897", "CorpusId": 293452876}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a3e524fa658b69f591ac9dd7720b51a4127a6063", "title": "When to Forget: Risk-Controlled Context Adaptation for Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22897, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362980894", "name": "Chen-Leng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2315861110", "name": "Yang-Bo Wei"}, {"authorId": "2514825973", "name": "Zhi-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2507870689", "name": "Zhen-Yu Su"}, {"authorId": "2260433198", "name": "Qi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2394493136", "name": "Jun-Hong Qian"}, {"authorId": "2454315569", "name": "Chao Yao"}, {"authorId": "2355015452", "name": "Chen Wu"}, {"authorId": "2265165404", "name": "Lei He"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon agents learn from failure, but the value of a failure record can change as execution progresses. We study when retaining, compressing, or removing the same history block improves subsequent execution relative to full-history KEEP. We introduce RECAP, a framework that combines matched context interventions, execution-relational prediction, and independent policy certification. Matched environment states and rollout seeds measure success and continuation prompt-cost differences; prefix-only relations between a failure block and the current execution propose promising edits. Independent task bundles then select a policy subject to harmful-intervention and non-positive-benefit risk constraints under a fixed measurement protocol. A five-seed longitudinal case holds both the failure block and its summary fixed: compression fails at an early state and becomes a cost-saving intervention at a later state. Across five backbones, RECAP produces positive paired-success saving estimates with backbone-specific reliability-efficiency tradeoffs. On Hunyuan-A13B, it edits 43.24% of retained checkpoints while matching KEEP's observed 91.89% continuation success and reducing unconditional mean continuation prompt cost by 4,619.8 tokens (2.13%). The framework provides an execution-level reference for deciding when to forget: retain history by its value to what comes next."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d8d4aeb074836ea2de4663f8317fb920b8d4b194", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23677", "CorpusId": 293546561}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d8d4aeb074836ea2de4663f8317fb920b8d4b194", "title": "Agentic Physics-Grounded Video Generation via Memory-Augmented In-Context Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23677, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2376054477", "name": "Xi-Kang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2325641579", "name": "Shao-Cong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2305500652", "name": "Yan-Qi Bao"}, {"authorId": "2510508479", "name": "Can Wang"}, {"authorId": "2302364861", "name": "Zheng Gu"}, {"authorId": "2510015753", "name": "Zheng-Qing Chen"}, {"authorId": "2307929487", "name": "Kai Chen"}, {"authorId": "2055851838", "name": "Jing Huo"}, {"authorId": "2375092142", "name": "Yang Gao"}], "abstract": "Physical plausibility is essential for realistic video generation. Existing methods impose physical guidance before synthesis via simulations or refined prompts, but this can limit dynamic diversity, miss errors that emerge only after generation, and require repeated regeneration when inconsistencies remain. We therefore study a generate-diagnose-repair paradigm that preserves the original generation capability and selectively repairs physical inconsistencies afterward, raising two key questions: what to repair and how to repair? To address them, we introduce PhysRepair, an agentic video generation framework that combines causal reasoning over the observed physical failure with prior repair experience to enable localized and adaptive correction. Specifically, Spatiotemporal Causal Constraints identify the first invalid physical state and its causally affected states, producing a repair specification that guides physically plausible local regeneration while preserving unaffected content. Experience-augmented In-context Learning combines the ongoing repair context with relevant experience from previous repairs, allowing the agent to adapt its repair decisions across diverse physical failures and accumulate completed trajectories for future cases. Experiments on Physics-IQ Verified across solid mechanics, fluid dynamics, optics, thermodynamics, and magnetism demonstrate that PhysRepair consistently improves the physical plausibility of generated videos over existing methods. Our project page is available at https://xxkkxxx.github.io/PhysRepair-Website/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5d524f69ae5a50ea3b1d3c975b8e156e8b8b5489", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23186", "CorpusId": 293545952}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5d524f69ae5a50ea3b1d3c975b8e156e8b8b5489", "title": "Code Skill Server: A Large-Scale Search Engine for Code Generation Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23186, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2515007307", "name": "Xiao-Yong Li"}, {"authorId": "2492104523", "name": "Hao-Long Li"}, {"authorId": "2378109439", "name": "Dong-Liang Xu"}, {"authorId": "2512690722", "name": "Hao-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2514731732", "name": "Yi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2515203264", "name": "Jing-Yuan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2473524993", "name": "Yang-Hua Wang"}, {"authorId": "2514731005", "name": "Yan-Bin Sun"}, {"authorId": "2338801282", "name": "Shi-Dong Pan"}, {"authorId": "2378112166", "name": "Yue Yao"}], "abstract": "Agent skills have proven to be an effective technique for improving agent ability on specific tasks, including software engineering, natural science, and mathematics. During such a process, using agent skills often involves manually discovering and organizing skills from heterogeneous sources, while existing skill retrieval methods mainly focus on selecting relevant skills from established skill libraries. However, with the rapidly increasing number and variety of reusable agent skills, manually searching for relevant skills across heterogeneous sources is becoming impractical at scale. In this paper, we introduce the Code Skill Server (CSS), a large-scale search engine for finding the most useful code generation skills for a target task. CSS consists of a skill server that incrementally collects and indexes publicly available code generation skills from multiple sources, with 993 skills included in the corpus used in this work. Extensive experiments across different retrieval methods and code generation benchmarks show that the best CSS configuration improves Pass@1 by up to 13.91% and consistently improves all evaluated models by at least 4.21% in the cross-model evaluation. These results demonstrate that CSS can effectively search for and serve relevant skills, improving code generation quality and enhancing models'code generation capabilities. Code and the CSS-993 corpus are available at {https://github.com/haoyuli-sdu/CodeSkillServer}."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "48c319021a5ca367b6e450f42f2ea659e2cf068a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23098", "CorpusId": 293546335}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/48c319021a5ca367b6e450f42f2ea659e2cf068a", "title": "InstAgent: A Recursively Self-Improving Agent Operating System for Heterogeneous Robot Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23098, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2333421879", "name": "Hao-Yang Luo"}, {"authorId": "2514693968", "name": "J. Qin"}, {"authorId": "2507477758", "name": "Jin-Yan Cao"}, {"authorId": "2500923891", "name": "Yi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2514397908", "name": "Guang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2515004138", "name": "Xiao-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2351826252", "name": "Hao-Tao Lu"}, {"authorId": "2363497754", "name": "Tian-Xiang Gui"}, {"authorId": "2192580886", "name": "Ye Shi"}, {"authorId": "2273021500", "name": "Jing-Ya Wang"}], "abstract": "Heterogeneous robot teams enable complex, long-horizon tasks by combining complementary sensing, mobility, and manipulation capabilities across multiple embodiments. Yet existing systems struggle to retain execution-time corrections as reusable experience, maintain task-relevant context memory across space and time for reliable handoffs, and explicitly represent the inter-robot dependencies required for structured collaboration. In order to address these challenges, we present InstAgent, a recursively self-improving (RSI) Agent Operating System for heterogeneous robot collaboration. InstAgent organizes team behaviors as agentic behavior trees (BTs) that explicitly model capability assignment, inter-robot dependencies and handoff checks. The RSI mechanism updates these collaboration programs from execution feedback and preserves successful revisions for reuse in future tasks, while shared multimodal spatio-temporal memory provides context for planning and handoff validation. Experiments on 20 real-world multi-robot tasks with three high-level models show consistent gains in task success, first-attempt completion, and execution efficiency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23574", "CorpusId": 293546054}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90ed2138c0c1c7c2c5310484c408126d5da21201", "title": "Darwin-Claw: Environment-Adaptive Experience Consolidation for Recursively Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23574, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297186228", "name": "Ming-Ze Yin"}, {"authorId": "2380043925", "name": "Xiao-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2316958318", "name": "Dian Li"}, {"authorId": "2515057402", "name": "Long-Fei Luo"}, {"authorId": "2362798356", "name": "Jia-Hong Yan"}, {"authorId": "2166681156", "name": "Yiheng Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2307736278", "name": "Chang-Yu Hsieh"}], "abstract": "While Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across complex tasks, they predominantly operate in isolated sessions, lacking the ability to continually learn from historical interactions or adapt to user-specific environments. Existing self-evolution paradigms often indiscriminately inject raw interaction trajectories into memory or skill modules, leading to obscured behavioral patterns and compromised update controllability. Furthermore, as foundational LLM reasoning improves, the bottleneck for agentic evolution shifts from accumulating generic knowledge to effectively managing situational experience. To address this, we propose Darwin-Claw, an environment-adaptive self-evolving framework that systematically distills episodic experiences to drive the comprehensive evolution of operational skills and durable memory. Theoretically grounded in an iterative agent-actor and evolver-critic co-adaptation, Darwin-Claw introduces a Hierarchical Experience Consolidation System that extracts fine-grained error attributions and generalizes them into dynamically updated prototype vectors. This structured situational experience serves as interaction patches for context-sensitive perception and explicit optimization signals for skill refinement. Practically, Darwin-Claw is a readily deployable, automated framework compatible with mainstream agent systems without requiring manual curation. Extensive experiments on WildClawBench demonstrate that Darwin-Claw yields substantial performance improvements, establishing a controllable and stable pathway for the continual, user-centric adaptation of autonomous agents in real-world ecosystems."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7d5e0a51384d0180e2cba203dfd8a55ad8d4b3e3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23128", "CorpusId": 293546164}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7d5e0a51384d0180e2cba203dfd8a55ad8d4b3e3", "title": "SCOPE: Selective Certification of Program Edits for Embodied Heuristic Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23128, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2401060254", "name": "Jun-Yang Shu"}, {"authorId": "2382845752", "name": "Bing-Qing Wei"}, {"authorId": "2267504431", "name": "Jinsong Li"}, {"authorId": "2281226931", "name": "Zhe-Qi He"}, {"authorId": "2380076557", "name": "Yong-Tao Wang"}], "abstract": "Embodied heuristic learning must balance strategic adaptation with the preservation of previously successful behavior. Local repairs may leave structural failures unresolved, whereas broader revisions can introduce regressions that are costly to detect through repeated environment interaction. We introduce SCOPE (Selective Certification Of Program Edits), which uses failed and successful executions as complementary evidence for program revision. Frontier-guided program search annotates a shared diagnostic graph with execution evidence to distinguish supported progress from unresolved checks. Selective behavioral certification uses stateful API replay to restore recorded responses and mutations, checking whether edits preserve selected historical successes. Diagnosis and certification feedback, supplemented by sparse environment diagnostics, guide consecutive repairs across configurations. SCOPE outperforms the strongest baseline in both simulation and real-world tasks. On LIBERO-PRO and Robosuite, it achieves mean success rates of 80.66% and 84.48%, with respective gains of 6.51 and 3.48 percentage points. Our code is available at https://github.com/VDIGPKU/SCOPE."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23288", "CorpusId": 293546354}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/00f2980617c653bd7b470cb428980cb4389c41b0", "title": "CoEvolve: Self-Evolving Code Policies from Skills to Strategies for Humanoid Robots", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23288, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2514746381", "name": "Zi-Cheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2119798830", "name": "Haochen Wu"}, {"authorId": "2514740104", "name": "Jia-Wei He"}, {"authorId": "2514851796", "name": "Su-Yun Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2454755594", "name": "Huan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2333875987", "name": "Cheng Chi"}, {"authorId": "2514966939", "name": "Hao Tang"}], "abstract": "Recent advances in Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models and World Action Models (WAMs) have expanded the manipulation and whole body capabilities of individual robots. Yet a single robot remains limited by its embodiment, and many real world tasks require capabilities that can only emerge through cooperation. Existing multi robot frameworks largely emphasize task decomposition, allocation, and replanning, while providing limited support for adapting executable coordination when physical execution deviates from the intended behavior. We present CoEvolve, a framework that represents team coordination as executable code over fixed robot motor skills. CoEvolve introduces Verifier Guided Coordination Evolution, where a multimodal foundation model instantiates coordination strategies through motion primitives, semantic objectives, temporary robot roles, and bounded execution parameters. Physical verification evaluates each executed transition, rejected behaviors trigger structured coordination revision from an accepted checkpoint, and verified experience is retained to guide subsequent decisions. We evaluate CoEvolve with two simulated Unitree G1 humanoids transporting a freely supported rigid payload through constrained corridors. Across 15 base task runs with three multimodal backbone families, CoEvolve completes 8 tasks, with every backbone achieving end to end success. Additional evaluations yield verified placement on two additional corridor layouts and after both same-backbone and cross-backbone experience initialization, while exposing persistent limitations in motion precision and contact stability. These results support executable coordination as a practical interface for adapting long horizon multi robot collaboration under shared physical constraints."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8d5aab147839ff408b07f6bd19edd1170c7d6ed2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24090", "CorpusId": 293687223}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8d5aab147839ff408b07f6bd19edd1170c7d6ed2", "title": "Persistent Memory and Iterative Improvement in an LLM Agent for Chinese-Character Drawing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24090, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510541248", "name": "Pei-Lin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2265763056", "name": "Yi-Zhuang You"}], "abstract": "Does persistent memory accumulated from previous attempts help an LLM agent solve new problems, and does memory design affect that benefit? We study programmatic Chinese-character drawing across five agent conditions attempting the same 668 items. Accumulated memory produces modest aggregate gains despite substantial growth in the memory stores: free-form notes improve first-attempt success by 4.6 percentage points, while a design combining free-form memory with reusable code improves it by 2.8 points. The two designs do not differ significantly overall, but the code bank shows a suggestive advantage on later, harder items. A separate post-hoc comparison finds a much larger improvement when the code-bank agent receives privileged, target-specific structural supervision. The agent can therefore exploit highly informative structural guidance, although the privileged per-item information does not establish what could have been learned from experience alone. These results suggest that memory design may affect where accumulated experience becomes useful, even when its average benefit is modest."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ba5896dd7b2037f571170607362496bb1aa6e4b0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24282", "CorpusId": 293686878}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ba5896dd7b2037f571170607362496bb1aa6e4b0", "title": "ROAR: Benchmarking LMM Agents under Real-Time Constraints in Multiplayer Action-Shooter Games", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24282, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2276968555", "name": "Da-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2258097326", "name": "Di Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2515482684", "name": "Jian-Ran Yu"}, {"authorId": "2111167774", "name": "Cheng-Min Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2404975498", "name": "Marci Chi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2473610033", "name": "Xiao-Yang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2323500396", "name": "Bei-Ning Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2154122548", "name": "Shuai Chu"}, {"authorId": "2284689031", "name": "Yun Sing Koh"}, {"authorId": "2557946", "name": "Gary Ushaw"}, {"authorId": "50653199", "name": "Richard Davison"}], "abstract": "Large multimodal models (LMMs) are increasingly deployed as agents that perceive, reason, and act in interactive multi-agent environments. Yet most existing benchmarks for LMM-based agents in multi-agent settings remain turn-based: the environment and other agents pause while an agent performs inference. Such protocols obscure a fundamental challenge of real-time constraints, where the world continues to evolve and delayed decisions may become outdated before execution. Motivated by this limitation, we introduce ROAR, a multiplayer action-shooter game environment for evaluating LMM-based agents in multi-agent settings under real-time constraints. ROAR covers cooperative, competitive, mixed cooperative-competitive, and basic single-agent scenarios. We further propose a Multi-Agent Asynchronous Gym interface that decouples environment evolution from agent reasoning by running the environment and each agent in separate threads, enabling the environment to advance at fixed time intervals without waiting for LMM inference and allowing agents to reason independently without blocking one another. ROAR also features convenient deployment, multimodal observations, and customizable levels. ROAR offers a practical testbed for advancing LMM-based agents beyond static, turn-based evaluation toward real-time multi-agent decision-making."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ee7f54b9eff911bf1efd2dc65eb6758b47bcb76d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23479", "CorpusId": 295588125}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ee7f54b9eff911bf1efd2dc65eb6758b47bcb76d", "title": "HexaShell: A Universal Harness for Robot Coding Agents in Simulation and the Real World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23479, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "2372582333", "name": "Hong-Cheng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2505370090", "name": "Jian-Shu Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2506017930", "name": "Alex Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2502939071", "name": "S. Ge"}, {"authorId": "2502850704", "name": "X. Shang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2105071435", "name": "Ya-Zhe Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506029000", "name": "Ye-Xing Wan"}, {"authorId": "2452617768", "name": "Ze Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2505769176", "name": "Jay Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2514928089", "name": "Xi-Ping Li"}, {"authorId": "2504358350", "name": "Di Wu"}, {"authorId": "2514675625", "name": "Yong-Li Wang"}, {"authorId": "2489547261", "name": "Ling-\u25b2-Yun-\u25bc He"}, {"authorId": "2505389153", "name": "Zhi-Qi Jia"}, {"authorId": "2505392818", "name": "Si-Hang Wu"}, {"authorId": "2502170800", "name": "Xiao He"}], "abstract": "Agents built on vision language models (VLMs) now control robots by writing code, but the strongest of them refine task-specific code over many rounds of trial and revision. Such code tends to overfit the benchmark on which it was tuned, can exploit the benchmark's shortcuts, and is costly to carry over to a new benchmark or robot. We present HexaShell, a thin, task-agnostic harness designed to test how far general tools can go instead. We design a simple and complete set of 14 basic tools, 4 for motion, 5 for perception and 5 for state, which the agent calls in one Python cell per turn. Perception pairs SAM 3, which segments an object from a few words or a click, with NumPy and OpenCV geometry that turns its outlines in several cameras, or a depth image, into its position, size and grasp angle. The API, agent loop and sandbox are shared across all tasks; a new simulator or robot needs only an adapter of 350 to 1,450 lines and a prompt describing its world, and the only task-specific input is an optional skill of about a page of guidance. The same code runs on four simulated benchmarks, built on three simulators with two kinds of arm, and on real robots. With GPT-6-Astra and no task-specific input, HexaShell reaches success rates of 69.9% on RoboDojo, 59.3% on RoboCasa365, 78.3% on LIBERO-PRO and 82.2% on RoboTwin 2.0. With task skills, it reaches 85.0% (score 88.8), 85.6%, 96.7% and 96.8%, respectively, above the best published result we are aware of on each benchmark. The RoboCasa365 result is listed first on its leaderboard. On real dual-arm robots, HexaShell succeeds in 76 of 90 episodes across nine tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3fd4cbb6796cfe85866381ac79b966ef65164ba9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22061", "CorpusId": 293004478}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3fd4cbb6796cfe85866381ac79b966ef65164ba9", "title": "FAOPs: Normal Forms and an Algebra for AI Agent Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22061, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2034659", "name": "Ali Mohammadi Shanghooshabad"}], "abstract": "Organizations increasingly encode operational procedures as multi-step agent skills, here called Agent Operating Procedures (AOPs), that must be traceable, auditable, reproducible, access-controlled, and bounded in time and cost, as regulated sectors require. Yet AOPs are typically kept as coarse-grained files, in which steps bundle actions, copied procedures drift apart, any step may reach any tool, and refactoring goes unchecked. We present Functional Agent Operating Procedures (FAOP), a formal theory that brings database-grade discipline to AI agent workflows. FAOP recasts each workflow as a typed function over scoped state and subjects an entire library to four progressive normal forms: Decomposed (FNF-1) splits a workflow into atomic steps, Typed (FNF-2) makes its data flow explicit and type-checked, Factored (FNF-3) gives each shared sub-workflow a single canonical definition so a change propagates instead of drifting, and Scoped (FNF-4) checks that each FAOP lists resources used by its own actions and the FAOPs it calls, while guardrails declared by it and by FAOPs that call it remain attached to their sources. A FAOP algebra of provably sound equalities turns refactoring and parallelization into compiler rewrites; orthogonal extensions add optimization (+O), bounded completion with time and cost limits (+B), and reachability checks (+R); and an architectural-level classifier (0-3) determines whether a workflow needs AI at all. Together, these make integrity, isolation, typing, stopping limits, cost, and optimization compile-time concerns across the whole library, not runtime hopes. We implement the theory in SkillBase, an AOP management system (what PostgreSQL is to data, SkillBase is to AOPs), and a separate executor that loads FAOPs from SkillBase and enforces these guarantees at run time. A worked financial workflow illustrates the approach end to end."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "82015d5fc53b587d5258d4d13dd9e3109cc0b56b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21113", "CorpusId": 292981783}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/82015d5fc53b587d5258d4d13dd9e3109cc0b56b", "title": "SSR: Structural Signal Retriever for Scalable Agent Discovery in LLM-Orchestrated Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21113, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2389335213", "name": "G. M. Cardenas"}, {"authorId": "3250914", "name": "Carlos A. Astudillo"}, {"authorId": "8394083", "name": "N. D. Da Fonseca"}], "abstract": "Large-scale multi-agent ecosystems require mechanisms for discovering suitable agents without exposing entire registries to a large language model (LLM) orchestrator. The challenge is not only to retrieve relevant Agent Cards from a growing registry, but also to maintain a compact candidate context, since each additional candidate increases prompt size, inference cost, latency, and the risk of exceeding the model context window. We formulate this problem as candidate-context scalability and propose the Structural Signal Retriever (SSR), our deterministic and training-free retrieval method designed for Agent Card discovery. SSR derives structural lexical signals from capability-bearing Agent Card fields, weights them using inverse document frequency (IDF), and uses an Inverted Signal Index (ISI) for selective candidate scoring. We evaluate SSR using a synthetic agent benchmark, a public MS MARCO adaptation, and end-to-end LLM orchestration. At 1,000 agents, SSR reduces LLM input by 98.8% and orchestration latency by 70.9% relative to full-catalog orchestration. On MS MARCO, IDF improves retrieval effectiveness within the structural representation, while the ISI preserves retrieval effectiveness and reduces explicitly scored passages by 96.8%. End-to-end experiments show that candidate pre-selection maintains a compact candidate context as registry size grows and avoids the operational failures observed with full-catalog orchestration. These results support SSR as an Agent-Card-native, traceable retrieval mechanism for candidate-context scalability."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fb1b5bc9aaad20ca80aaabdfb68347e834dcfb88", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21165", "CorpusId": 292981875}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fb1b5bc9aaad20ca80aaabdfb68347e834dcfb88", "title": "From Long Context to Selective Memory:A Survey of Memory-Centric Compression for Large Language Models", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21165, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "1491637173", "name": "Chao Gou"}], "abstract": "Long-context capability has become essential for large language models (LLMs) to handle complex tasks, yet continuously expanding the context window does not remove the computational, storage, and information-selection pressures incurred at inference time. Existing work on prompt compression, KV-cache management, efficient serving, retrieval augmentation, and agent memory is usually discussed in isolation, leaving the flow of information between storage and computation insufficiently understood. This survey reframes long-context compression as a memory-management problem for allocating inference-time resources. We distinguish compute-active memory, which directly participates in model computation, from compute-inactive memory, which lies outside the computation path but can be stored, retrieved, updated, or forgotten, and we characterize offloaded KV caches, persistent prefix caches, and precomputed KV states as intermediate states on the activation path. On this basis, we formulate writing, offloading, recall, retrieval-and-injection, materialization, eviction, consolidation, and forgetting as state transitions within an information lifecycle, and introduce a budgeted cost model that places these operations in a single cost account. The resulting framework unifies short-term context compression, long-term memory management, and the transitions between them, and shows that the central challenge is not merely reducing information volume but deciding which information should participate in costly inference, when, and in what form. We further summarize evaluation dimensions, benchmark gaps, and open challenges, and argue that future research should move from static, pointwise compression toward dynamic, governable, lifecycle-aware memory management."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c9a41b588537015995c7cf0ba731c58a2ff814b3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22362", "CorpusId": 293004214}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c9a41b588537015995c7cf0ba731c58a2ff814b3", "title": "CoDA: Co-Evolution of Data Engines and Action Policies", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22362, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297153733", "name": "Tianjiao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2511794943", "name": "Zhi-Lin Ou"}, {"authorId": "2329388208", "name": "Yi-Fan Shen"}, {"authorId": "2336119887", "name": "Xin-Zhuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2371326711", "name": "Yuan-Zhe Liu"}, {"authorId": "2149412487", "name": "O. Susladkar"}, {"authorId": "2512331284", "name": "Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2099420", "name": "Ismini Lourentzou"}], "abstract": "Generative simulation can automate robot environment construction, but physically valid environments are not necessarily useful for training. The challenge is to identify environments that address a policy's failures and supervision that helps it overcome them. We introduce CoDA, a framework in which a simulation data engine and an action policy co-evolve through a shared heterogeneous memory of environment construction, policy failures, and learning progress. The memory compiles validated diagnoses from a vision-language critic into generation programs that recreate difficulties and training programs that help the policy overcome them. We further propose Text2potential to convert these diagnoses into staged potentials for action-chunk value learning, providing dense supervision for policy updates while preserving the task's optimal policy under standard shaping assumptions. Measured learning progress guides further generation and program refinement. Across four manipulation task families, CoDA improves out-of-distribution success from 5.3% after supervised fine-tuning to 27.6%, exceeding the strongest closed-loop baseline by 9.5 percentage points with the same generation budget. On LIBERO-Pro, 15 rounds of generation and training raise success from 25% to 84% under position perturbations and from 1% to 76% under instruction perturbations."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2b4c6b2ba5c48810d108da02522f37ea9112a89a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21453", "CorpusId": 293003558}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2b4c6b2ba5c48810d108da02522f37ea9112a89a", "title": "CarryOn: A Dual-Loop Harness for Personalized and Reliable Robot Assistance", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21453, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2322451250", "name": "Zixuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2334922159", "name": "Yu-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2362317400", "name": "Yu-Xin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2276746547", "name": "Sen-Qiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2515561008", "name": "Jin-Hui Ye"}, {"authorId": "7778612", "name": "Peng-Guang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2251047028", "name": "Chang-Sheng Lu"}, {"authorId": "2278256143", "name": "Shu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2349958453", "name": "Bei Yu"}, {"authorId": "2273012826", "name": "Jia-Ya Jia"}], "abstract": "While generalist vision-language-action (VLA) models have enabled diverse open-world manipulation, real-world robotic assistance requires two critical capabilities they currently lack: personalization and reliable execution. First, executing an instruction like\"make me a sandwich\"requires grounding subtask decomposition in persistent user preferences rather than relying on a single generic solution. Second, reliable deployment demands explainable safety assurance that proactively prevents hazards and gracefully recovers from occasional physical failures. To address these gaps, we present CarryOn, a dual-loop harness designed to augment a frozen VLA policy. The planning loop maintains persistent user-level and scene-level context that is continually grounded against new observations and revised through interaction, enabling personalized task selection and execution grounding across conversations and changing scenes. The execution loop actively supervises policy rollouts, shielding actions from predicted hazardous contact. When execution fails, the system invokes reusable control-based recovery skills that restore task progress and are refined through reflection on earlier failures. On a real robot and in simulation, CarryOn dispatches preference-consistent subtasks across users and scenes, reduces the violation rate of an already safety-finetuned VLA from 52.6% to 6.1%, and increases recovery success from 13% to 80% when refined skills are reused."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "28f53bd88c8bf34d527b0fda596cc7b8ab199749", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21492", "CorpusId": 293003768}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/28f53bd88c8bf34d527b0fda596cc7b8ab199749", "title": "MetaExp: Learning to Self-Improve through Experience via Verifiable Cross-Task Environment Scaling", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21492, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2303429731", "name": "Ren-Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2471847272", "name": "Xin-Jie Shen"}, {"authorId": "2389123157", "name": "Yang Su"}, {"authorId": "2321581897", "name": "Jianhong Tu"}, {"authorId": "2303402020", "name": "Derek F. Wong"}, {"authorId": "2403252635", "name": "Dayiheng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2346068784", "name": "Min Yang"}], "abstract": "Language-model agents can accumulate interaction feedback, but reliably turning that feedback into reusable experience remains challenging. Existing methods either optimize external experience scaffolds or train experience-related behavior on specific task distributions, leaving open whether experience learning itself can become a transferable model capability. Training such a capability calls for supervision beyond isolated tasks, which mainly support in-episode problem solving rather than revealing what knowledge remains useful across episodes. In this work, we introduce MetaExp, a scalable framework that trains this capability through verifiable task families with controlled cross-task structure. Each family preserves a reusable factor while varying task-specific realizations, enabling transfer without direct solution reuse. We propose two complementary designs: Shared-Tool families keep black-box tools fixed across tasks to elicit operational experience, while Shared-Workflow families preserve latent procedures across changing realizations to elicit procedural experience. We scale environments under both designs, collect and filter sequential interaction trajectories, and fine-tune Qwen3.5-122B-A10B on 4,956 trajectories from 1,000 task families. With parameters frozen during downstream evolution, MetaExp improves by +8.5 points on SpreadsheetBench, peaks at +2.3 on APEX-Agents, and improves by +14.8 on AutomationBench. The gains persist under domain-excluded training, supporting experience learning as a transferable capability for continued non-parametric improvement after deployment."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3194c3d49ef6dbcf5f9a2ae33e2c0df85d6f2f09", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21646", "CorpusId": 293004060}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3194c3d49ef6dbcf5f9a2ae33e2c0df85d6f2f09", "title": "The Self-Improvement Problem", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21646, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2064375154", "name": "Brian B. Moser"}, {"authorId": "50120446", "name": "Stanislav Frolov"}, {"authorId": "2232602491", "name": "T. Nauen"}, {"authorId": "48258541", "name": "Federico Raue"}, {"authorId": "2282138873", "name": "Andreas Dengel"}], "abstract": "With the rise of large language models, agentic systems are becoming increasingly autonomous, as they write code, call tools, revise prompts, and propose modifications to their own components. This autonomy raises a fundamental question for safe self-improvement: can an automated gate like a reward model or an automated reviewer reliably decide whether a proposed update should be accepted? The difficulty is structural. The gate must be a terminating, computable procedure, while the property we ultimately care about, namely whether an update is a genuine semantic improvement, depends on potentially unbounded program behavior. We formalize this mismatch by modeling agents as programs and defining improvement in terms of a semantic score that need not be computable. We study two evaluation abstractions. The first is a certifier that approves or rejects an update. The second is a search procedure that, given access to a proposal generator, attempts to output some improving candidate if one exists. We show that, in the fully general setting, no terminating procedure can simultaneously guarantee reliability (never accepting a non-improvement) and non-conservatism (never missing an available improvement): self-referential constructions can force any such gate or searcher to fail. Randomized gates with bounded error face the same limit. We then identify a standard decidable regime used in practice (bounded evaluation and a finite proposal budget) under which exhaustive simulation yields a terminating search procedure with the desired guarantees. We also show the limit of this recovery: a chain of accepted updates can raise the bounded score in every round while the true score falls. Our results draw a boundary between unbounded, universal settings where correctness guarantees collapse and bounded settings where they can be recovered by design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0b6fb3c5f486a70c7cc58bc9ef76d0a8f9a2ba58", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22280", "CorpusId": 293004293}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0b6fb3c5f486a70c7cc58bc9ef76d0a8f9a2ba58", "title": "LEASH: Uncertainty-Aware Weak-to-Strong Escalation and Oversight for Computer-Use Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22280, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2309196873", "name": "Qing-Ni Wang"}, {"authorId": "2152046120", "name": "Changdae Oh"}, {"authorId": "2316673843", "name": "Yuzhe Yang"}, {"authorId": "2385968241", "name": "Sharon Li"}, {"authorId": "2325193568", "name": "Jia-Chen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2325491830", "name": "Ang Li"}, {"authorId": "2325785593", "name": "X. Wang"}], "abstract": "Computer-use agents increasingly tackle long-horizon tasks, where running a frontier model at every step is expensive and a small open-weight model alone often fails. Weak-to-strong escalation offers a middle ground where a weak agent acts by default and hands control to a strong one only when needed. The core challenge is deciding when to escalate, which requires an uncertainty signal cheap enough to check at every step. However, even after handoff, the strong agent can still get lost, but a black-box API that the strong agent is usually served exposes no internals to monitor. To this end, we propose LEASH, a training-free framework that unleashes the strong agent only when the weak agent's latent uncertainty signals it is needed, and keeps it on a leash afterward. LEASH has three layers: (1) initial task uncertainty for early routing, (2) step-level uncertainty for runtime escalation, and, after handoff, (3) a shadow observer for continued oversight of the strong black-box agent. Here, we use latent uncertainty as a fuel for all three layers. Specifically, we compare the weak model's hidden states with those from its past successful and failed trajectories, and a state that looks more like the failures signals higher uncertainty. During weak-agent execution, our uncertainty estimator reuses activations already produced during action generation, so it needs no extra forward pass or dedicated monitor training. Experiments on OSWorld across four contemporary model backbones show that LEASH consistently improves over standalone weak agents and outperforms random escalation by 6.55--11.94 points at matched escalation rates. With Qwen3.5-35B-A3B, LEASH achieves 73.66% task success at $0.4117 per task, matching always-on Claude Opus 5 (72.90%) while reducing inference cost by 19.2%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3c5c84487179b0fd24c07eaac9fffbef4a3227e3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21427", "CorpusId": 293004557}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3c5c84487179b0fd24c07eaac9fffbef4a3227e3", "title": "Learning from Interaction Experience via Localized Context Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21427, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347924913", "name": "Zhe-Zheng Hao"}, {"authorId": "2469401065", "name": "Yong-Kang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2495058844", "name": "Xia-Ling-Zhang-Yu-Xin Xia"}, {"authorId": "7177756", "name": "Jiarui Yu"}, {"authorId": "2372205286", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2457015304", "name": "Fan Wei"}, {"authorId": "2265581222", "name": "Pei-Jie Dong"}, {"authorId": "2469116813", "name": "Tian-Fu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2377534360", "name": "Qiang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2279089364", "name": "Can Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371298953", "name": "Han-De Dong"}, {"authorId": "2385486198", "name": "Jia-Wei Chen"}], "abstract": "Learning from interaction experience is essential for enabling large language models to continually improve during deployment. Context distillation offers a promising path: privileged information extracted from experience is provided to a teacher, whose behavior is then distilled into a student model that has no access to it. However, both existing forms of context distillation have limitations. Off-policy context distillation trains the student on teacher-generated trajectories and thus suffers from exposure bias; whereas on-policy context distillation trains on the student's own rollouts, whose states are often uninformative and fail to convey behaviors the student cannot yet produce. To resolve this dilemma, we introduce Localized Context Distillation (LoCD). LoCD first localizes the critical spans of a student trajectory that require revision. The teacher then regenerates only these spans from the student's own prefix, and the student is trained on the regenerated segments. LoCD thereby concentrates learning on the most informative states while keeping exposure bias low. Extensive experiments across math reasoning, code generation, and agent tasks demonstrate that LoCD consistently outperforms strong baselines and achieves more stable training with smaller and sparser parameter updates."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e360bc2d9a1d574d374949eb4cab0dbcdcb18b2e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22289", "CorpusId": 293003900}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e360bc2d9a1d574d374949eb4cab0dbcdcb18b2e", "title": "Improving Environment-Agnostic Agents: The Role of Scaffolding", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22289, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2307358073", "name": "Sergey Rodionov"}], "abstract": "Agents built on stronger language models may increasingly construct useful procedures for themselves, reducing the advantage of developer-supplied scaffolding. We investigate why this advantage persists and whether an environment-agnostic agent can be improved without environment-specific engineering. We evaluate a Codex-based agent in two nearly identical prompt variants on ARC-AGI-3, AGI Maze, and Terminal-Bench. Each variant is deployed with unchanged code, prompts, and general-purpose tools across environments, receiving environment-specific information as input. Changing only an instruction to encourage reporting difficulties and returning control for automatic continuation improves mean scores on both ARC-AGI-3 and AGI Maze, while Terminal-Bench shows small, mixed changes. Yet the general agent remains below an ARC-AGI-3-specific reference, including with GPT-5.6-sol. The reference prescribes organization and control rather than supplying new game rules or solutions. To investigate this gap, we transfer reset decisions to a scripted controller that follows a convention already given to the agent. This small change recovers part of the specialized agent's advantage. A complementary AGI Maze experiment shows that additional tutorial experience can reduce performance. Together, these findings highlight the distinction between having information and tools available and using them effectively. Supplied procedures still matter for the models tested here. The performance gains also come with higher token-based cost. The experiments do not separate better use of information and procedures from additional or differently allocated inference. We provide the EnvTraverse framework and an evaluated baseline agent for testing whether further environment-independent changes improve performance in some environments without degrading it in others."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a8ec8092f9b1e0b3994638076291a9d949c4247f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22319", "CorpusId": 293004109}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a8ec8092f9b1e0b3994638076291a9d949c4247f", "title": "A team of Gemini 4 Argon agents beats Minecraft", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22319, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2210859817", "name": "Maxence Faldor"}, {"authorId": "2108384528", "name": "Satinder Singh"}, {"authorId": "48647153", "name": "R. Raileanu"}], "abstract": "We placed four Gemini 4 Argon agents in a sandboxed Minecraft survival world and gave them one goal: defeat the Ender Dragon. Each agent controlled a player via an open-source library called Mineflayer and coordinated with its teammates through in-game chat and shared memory. No one assigned roles or wrote game logic. The agents planned, wrote, and debugged every program themselves. Researchers could watch the run on a live dashboard and pause or stop it at any time, but they had no cause to intervene. Starting from a randomly generated world on Easy difficulty, the team wrote 2,783 programs and defeated the dragon in 27 hours and 14 minutes, dying 605 times along the way. To our knowledge, this is the first end-to-end autonomous completion of Minecraft by AI agents, achieved using only the standard Mineflayer library without pre-scouted seeds or other game-specific tools written by humans. Winning required the agents to keep a coherent plan across thousands of dependent steps for more than a day. Complex real-world software engineering and enterprise tasks demand the same long-horizon coding and multi-agent coordination. We describe the setup, how the run unfolded, the team's strategies (dividing the work, sharing what they learned, recovering each other's items), and the limits of the result."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bd55b40184229d1b0a04dc21148b49ba7c258206", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22686", "CorpusId": 293452778}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bd55b40184229d1b0a04dc21148b49ba7c258206", "title": "Multimodal Filesystem Memory For Long-Horizon Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22686, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2390197675", "name": "C. Su"}, {"authorId": "2507877074", "name": "Jia-Hang Li"}, {"authorId": "2398622512", "name": "Zhen-Ting Qi"}, {"authorId": "2267385326", "name": "Chao-Ran Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2514810516", "name": "Hao-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2387973381", "name": "Sejoon Jun"}, {"authorId": "83546634", "name": "Wei-Rui Ye"}, {"authorId": "2383299859", "name": "Yi-Lun Du"}], "abstract": "Agents operating over long horizons need an effective memory that allows them to reuse their experience throughout long interactions. If the entire history of an interaction is retained, it becomes difficult to find the relevant experience, while compressing it into summaries may result in the loss of visual and spatial details that are required later on. We present \\textbf{MFM}, a multimodal filesystem memory architecture that keeps complementary types of agent memory. \\textbf{Episodic memory} stores the observations, actions, and outcomes, and \\textbf{spatial memory} associates experiences with locations. \\textbf{Semantic and procedural memory} build up general knowledge and reusable methods of acting, and \\textbf{working memory} holds the current plans. MFM makes use of searchable textual records to connect these different representations to the underlying multimodal episodes. The agents are able to find previous experiences by means of contextual cues and to examine the original observations in order to recover details that are missing from their textual descriptions. Through consolidation, the agents convert experience into higher-level knowledge and procedures while still keeping references to the original episodes. In the case of long-horizon Minecraft and computer-use tasks, MFM leads to improved performance with three base models. With GPT-6-Astra, MFM completes \\emph{Mine Diamond from Scratch} and reaches 16 out of 24 milestones on the \\emph{Ender Dragon} task, as against 12.7 for the best baseline. On CUABench-KiCad it attains a mean reward of 0.440, as compared to 0.400 for the best baseline and 0.267 in the case of no persistent memory."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22561", "CorpusId": 293453025}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/662e9bd962d801a390a02ebf868bfb15575eb28d", "title": "FRACTAL: A Self-Similar Multi-Agent Orchestration Architecture with Formal Communication, Convergence, and Depth Bounds", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22561, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "10122218", "name": "Ohad Dan"}, {"authorId": "2514439267", "name": "Abhinav Taliyan"}], "abstract": "We present FRACTAL, a self-similar multi-agent architecture for recursive goal decomposition, in which the same five-component organizational pattern -- Orchestrator, Ensemble, Knowledge Ledger, Decision Maker, Meta-Observer -- recurs at every level of a decomposition tree. We make four formal contributions: (1) a communication bound showing intra-node cost is linear in ensemble size and round count (O(kr)) under a two-phase blind-proposal/stress-test protocol, versus quadratic cost for free-form debate, with an explicit, stated assumption on fixed evaluator count; (2) a two-mode orchestration criterion separating generative and evaluative phases via a measurable diversity-saturation threshold; (3) a supermajority convergence guarantee with explicit probability bounds at stated agreement levels, and a formal depth bound under an operationalized goal-complexity measure; (4) a Bayesian inter-layer escalation mechanism unifying hysteresis and contract-breach signaling under one posterior update. We illustrate the architecture's execution qualitatively on a worked example and a multi-layer case study; we do not report quantitative performance claims in this paper, and we explicitly discuss the formal and inductive-bias limitations of the model, including an unimplemented but architecture-compatible direction toward bounded self-improvement. We use\"fractal\"as an architectural metaphor for self-similarity, not a measured mathematical property. Empirical validation of the architecture's comparative performance is left to future work."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d87329f468319843890f23f1babd4b164c3cd26c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22732", "CorpusId": 293453300}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d87329f468319843890f23f1babd4b164c3cd26c", "title": "TokenEconomizer: Experience-Driven Black-Box Cost Control for Coding Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22732, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2299473032", "name": "Aaron J. Li"}, {"authorId": "2266751372", "name": "Wei-Jia Xu"}, {"authorId": "2261286637", "name": "C. Singh"}, {"authorId": "2404540974", "name": "Zi-Xiao Chen"}, {"authorId": "1827015", "name": "J. Inala"}, {"authorId": "2364686196", "name": "Charlotte Siska"}, {"authorId": "2514443579", "name": "Acar Tamersoy"}, {"authorId": "2346813317", "name": "Bin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2290576964", "name": "Ion Stoica"}, {"authorId": "2253458981", "name": "Michel Galley"}, {"authorId": "2296412141", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Advances in frontier coding agents have sharply increased the token costs of AI-assisted software engineering. Their token efficiency is typically evaluated on isolated tasks, whereas real-world developers use them to continuously build, update, and maintain large-scale projects. This mismatch motivates us to evaluate agents over sequences of realistic coding tasks, where we find that agents exhibit recurring environment-dependent token costs that existing methods struggle to reduce. To minimize these costs, we introduce TokenEconomizer, an external controller that cuts environment-specific token costs over a sequence of tasks. These cuts come from determining (i) which operational micro-skills to inject, (ii) which persistently irrelevant tools to prune, and (iii) when to intervene in unproductive failure loops. Across Claude Code, Codex, and GitHub Copilot CLI paired with frontier models, TokenEconomizer reduces the average token cost by 18.4-30.9% with comparable task success. Savings hold in every repository under every tested agent-model setting and generalize to diverse domains such as sequential web development and security auditing, where costs fall by 6.4-21.2% depending on task structure. Our work establishes adaptive black-box cost control as a promising direction for reducing token costs of coding agents in realistic settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6fb748db583b9ac548f1ed8e8bf55cb13621023e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19843", "CorpusId": 292902321}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6fb748db583b9ac548f1ed8e8bf55cb13621023e", "title": "LAGO: Language Agents with Reusable Growing Options", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19843, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2500250331", "name": "Yu-Ran Wu"}, {"authorId": "2364560731", "name": "Meng-Kang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2400967514", "name": "Q. Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2277883005", "name": "Fan-Qing Meng"}, {"authorId": "2346067745", "name": "Qi-Guang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2295681570", "name": "Yu-Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "30024940", "name": "Qiu-Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2509715990", "name": "Xu Wang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon tasks expose a weakness of monolithic tool-use loops: one agent must decompose, execute, and verify a task within a single trajectory. We introduce LAGO, which treats specialized language agents as reusable temporally extended options. A meta-agent retrieves options from a persistent library or spawns new ones, dispatches sub-tasks, verifies results, and admits successful novel options. The library grows across tasks without updating model weights. On 500 SWE-bench Verified tasks, frozen Qwen3.5-35B-A3B in both roles resolves 58.6% under single-attempt evaluation with the official Docker evaluator. On a fixed 150-task subset, we re-run mini-SWE-agent, Terminus 2, and LAGO with the same policy, temperature, task IDs, and evaluator; their resolve rates are 49.3%, 57.3%, and 60.3%, respectively. Cross-model experiments provide evidence consistent with a role for decomposition quality. A 50-task component ablation finds four fewer resolved tasks without decomposition, two fewer with shuffled reused content, and six fewer without the library. These results support reusable agent-options as a practical abstraction for task streams, while leaving component-level causality task- and model-dependent."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b208fc40a451dddc8b427f8c2205f1da6b74156d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20240", "CorpusId": 292902908}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b208fc40a451dddc8b427f8c2205f1da6b74156d", "title": "PAWA: Panoramic Action-conditioned World Modeling for Multiple Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20240, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2206558642", "name": "Si-Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2259866135", "name": "Yimu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2314783462", "name": "Zi-Qi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2205928142", "name": "Cheng-Xuan Zhu"}, {"authorId": "46223195", "name": "Shu-Chen Weng"}, {"authorId": "2351080333", "name": "Erwin Wu"}, {"authorId": "2224390928", "name": "Zhao-Fei Yu"}, {"authorId": "2349384231", "name": "Kai-Peng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2150089067", "name": "Bo-Xin Shi"}], "abstract": "A multiplayer world model must predict how the independent actions of several players change a shared environment, including changes outside a player's current field of view. A point-of-view (POV) video captures such changes only after they enter its field of view, whereas panoramic video records the surroundings in every viewing direction and has served as an intermediate representation of the world state in recent world models. Learning this task therefore requires synchronized POV and panoramic videos of interacting players together with their actions, yet no existing dataset provides this combination in Minecraft, an established testbed in which players act independently in a shared, modifiable 3D world. We present PAWA, a dataset and framework for action-conditioned panoramic multiplayer world modeling. The dataset pairs each player's POV video with world-stabilized panoramic video and aligned actions. Its distributed pipeline decouples CPU gameplay capture from GPU rendering, scales beyond 64 simultaneous players, and re-renders each recorded session under new observation configurations without replaying the gameplay. On this dataset, we develop a dual-tower model that jointly predicts POV and panoramic video and couples the two streams through cross-attention that aligns tokens observing the same direction in a shared world frame. We further introduce a quadratic-phase player encoding that provides many more player codes than existing rotary codes at the same embedding dimension. Experiments show that PAWA outperforms state-of-the-art multiplayer world models in POV prediction quality while also producing high-fidelity panoramic predictions. The dataset and collection pipeline are open source to support further research on multiplayer world models."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0f11cb71be9ece66cf66aa30348912d50650d2a9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20560", "CorpusId": 292903117}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0f11cb71be9ece66cf66aa30348912d50650d2a9", "title": "PhySGA: A Physics-Space Grounded Agentic Framework for Generalizable and Interactive 3D Composition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20560, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348941463", "name": "Weichen Lyu"}, {"authorId": "2257004568", "name": "Chong Xia"}, {"authorId": "2407234534", "name": "Ming-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2158596679", "name": "Long-Ji Xu"}, {"authorId": "2293868235", "name": "Sheng-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2295741834", "name": "Yue-Qi Duan"}], "abstract": "3D object composition is pivotal to digital content creation and robot simulation, while also serving as a critical testbed for physical intelligence in foundation models. Previous methods either leverage large language models to directly predict object coordinates, or utilize generative diffusion priors to synthesize composite images for visual alignment, both of which rely on static priors in digital spaces. However, these methods consistently struggle with complex compositions such as precise hanging, gravity-driven settling and hidden containment, as these tasks require interactive construction processes involving global planning, fine-grained alignment, and physical simulation. To address these challenges, we propose PhySGA, a Physics-Space Grounded Agentic Framework for 3D composition via agent exploration and physical simulation, enabling the universal interactive assembly of rigid, articulated, and deformable objects. Directly applying vanilla LLMs or VLMs to this task proves insufficient, as current models inherently lack grounded physical understanding and interactive construction experience. To overcome this limitation, we design a collaborative feedforward-feedback architecture: an Assembly Agent focuses on geometric and kinematic reasoning for global step planning and coarse adjustment, while a Critic Agent reasons over contact dynamics for simulator state diagnosis and fine-grained closed-loop correction. Furthermore, Action Routers map semantic planning and analysis onto executable physical primitives within the simulator, while a task-specific Self-Evolving Skill Library distills historical trial-and-error feedback into reusable experiences, boosting success rates and minimizing exploration attempts. Extensive experiments within Isaac Sim demonstrate that PhySGA significantly surpasses existing methods in relation fidelity, physical plausibility, and visual quality."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "89a674194f28cff61198826066e4fc02a19f698f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19686", "CorpusId": 292902519}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/89a674194f28cff61198826066e4fc02a19f698f", "title": "Interlock: From Local Correctness to Task Completion in Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19686, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2348311347", "name": "Lin-Ji Hao"}, {"authorId": "2510805115", "name": "Yan-Ji He"}, {"authorId": "2510472394", "name": "Zi-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2406893596", "name": "Han-Si Yang"}, {"authorId": "2504338178", "name": "Jia-Yuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378579938", "name": "Hong-Yan Hao"}, {"authorId": "2383008230", "name": "Qi Gu"}, {"authorId": "2412097369", "name": "Yan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2453958864", "name": "Hank Wu"}], "abstract": "Large language model agents increasingly undertake long-horizon tasks in complex environments characterized by fragmented evidence, partially completed states, and dependencies across resources and artifacts. Existing agents may satisfy individual requirements while failing to maintain these relationships, producing locally correct but incomplete outcomes. These failures arise because task completion depends on relationships among environmental evidence, consequential decisions, and required effects. To address this completion gap, we propose Interlock, a framework for task synthesis that represents each task as a typed completion graph. Grounding edges connect public evidence to decisions, coupling hyperedges impose joint constraints across components, and realization edges connect decisions to all required downstream effects. Interlock compiles this graph into a resettable workspace, a public request, and an evaluation rubric. A source-coverage review checks whether the original requirements survive generation, while a public-input review checks whether the rubric is supported by the materials available to the solver. An agent-as-judge inspects execution artifacts and runs verification scripts, retaining trajectories judged to satisfy all mandatory requirements. To evaluate training utility, we fine-tune two Qwen3.6 backbones on their own accepted trajectories. Across the two backbones, training improves AutomationBench task pass rates by 4.00-4.50 percentage points, JobBench weighted scores by 3.20-6.64 points, and Harvey LAB criterion pass rates by 3.89-4.16 percentage points. These results demonstrate the training utility of the synthesized experience across external task environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "52054481227602b1e6919066a33b3689b3c1d306", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19882", "CorpusId": 292903605}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/52054481227602b1e6919066a33b3689b3c1d306", "title": "BluePixel: Toward Native 4K Agentic Image Editing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19882, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "51303943", "name": "Shan-Yan Guan"}, {"authorId": "2303281161", "name": "Shi-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2379643032", "name": "Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2350680556", "name": "Zhi-Zhou Chen"}, {"authorId": "2350744574", "name": "Yun-Zhe Xu"}, {"authorId": "30551953", "name": "Yue-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "47215940", "name": "Rui-Xue Shen"}, {"authorId": "2479406792", "name": "Shi-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2465571478", "name": "Min-Da Jin"}, {"authorId": "2510656532", "name": "Qiang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2365969624", "name": "Jun-Bo Liu"}, {"authorId": "2367196849", "name": "Peng Shi"}, {"authorId": "2494263917", "name": "Sheng-Ke Sun"}, {"authorId": "2352220179", "name": "Xiao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2509367410", "name": "Yong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2539841", "name": "Zhong-Ang Qi"}, {"authorId": "2325153056", "name": "Wei Li"}, {"authorId": "2262963387", "name": "Chao Ma"}, {"authorId": "2325161493", "name": "Ying Tai"}, {"authorId": "2325723271", "name": "Yan-Hao Ge"}], "abstract": "Instruction-driven image editing has reached native 4K, yet how such editors are trained remains undisclosed, and pretraining one from scratch is beyond most teams. The task itself compounds the difficulty: a single end-to-end editor cannot make precise parametric adjustments, match specialized experts, or interpret the vague instructions users actually write. We argue that 4K editing is a system problem rather than a single-model problem, and present BluePixel, which couples a native-4K editing model with an agent harness; the complete training recipe is disclosed in this paper. The model makes 4K training practical: it turns a 1K-pretrained editor into a native-4K one through progressive VAE adaptation that keeps 4K training as affordable as 1K, absorbs a long tail of vertical editing skills through continual learning, and refines the aesthetic-fidelity trade-off with reinforcement learning. Distillation via source-anchored target projection (SATP) then compresses 50 denoising steps to 4, completing a 4K edit in 8.7 s on a single Kunlunxin P800 XPU while preserving task success rate and background consistency. The harness, BluePixel Agent, makes editing controllable: it decomposes user instructions into specialist and general operations, verifies every result with the same reward model that guided training, and retries within bounded attempts. On VINS-4KEval++ (3,305 native-4K cases), BluePixel ranks second among seven models, behind only GPT Image 2 in non-edited-region consistency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d7ba9ec241f37c28ff5837ded21a8145c235ba78", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20424", "CorpusId": 292902940}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d7ba9ec241f37c28ff5837ded21a8145c235ba78", "title": "BizAgentBench: Unveiling Hidden Challenges in Deploying Long-Horizon Agents for Real-World Business", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2441892738", "name": "E. Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2260336914", "name": "Yong-Qi Tong"}, {"authorId": "2510653970", "name": "Xue-Jun Yu"}, {"authorId": "2450638101", "name": "Hang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2381925213", "name": "Pan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510649875", "name": "Yi-Chao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510715072", "name": "Di He"}, {"authorId": "2369357651", "name": "Jian-She Li"}, {"authorId": "2446870658", "name": "Jiang-Ming Yang"}, {"authorId": "2290396574", "name": "Wei Wu"}], "abstract": "Existing long-horizon agent benchmarks often emphasize final task success while providing limited assessment of intermediate execution validity. Yet real-world business operations require agents to navigate extensive, potentially outdated or conflicting policies while satisfying critical constraints throughout execution. These requirements are particularly consequential in high-stakes domains such as payments, where intermediate violations can incur financial losses or regulatory consequences regardless of the final outcome. To bridge this evaluation gap, we introduce BizAgentBench, a multi-turn benchmark grounded in real-world procedures from a leading payments company. Its executable environment integrates 789 knowledge documents and 601 discoverable business tools, with consistent policies, states, and tool behavior. The benchmark comprises 50 tasks involving resource constraints, temporal dependencies, prior commitments, and conflicting or outdated guidance. Each task is validated through reference execution, while deterministic checks assess both goal attainment and execution validity without prescribing a single solution path. Experiments reveal substantial limitations even among frontier models: Claude Opus 5 and GPT-6 Sol achieve strict success rates of only 64.0% and 61.3%, respectively. For Qwen3.8-27B, goal attainment reaches 42.0%, but strict success drops to 32.7%, demonstrating how outcome-based evaluation can mask invalid execution. Beyond success rates, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash averages 191.3 tool calls per run, approximately 70% more than reference executions, revealing substantial execution overhead. Overall, BizAgentBench establishes a testbed for evaluating and advancing long-horizon agents beyond task completion, toward reliable and constraint-compliant execution in complex business environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19951", "CorpusId": 292903495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "title": "AtomSearch: Evidence-Grounded Tree Search for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497901572", "name": "Jiang-Shuai-Chen-Rui-,-Zhang-Rong-,-Wang-Gui-Fang- Tong"}, {"authorId": "2430689890", "name": "Sen Deng"}, {"authorId": "2469306138", "name": "Ming-Pei Peng"}, {"authorId": "2311866886", "name": "Jian-Feng Pan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills adapt frozen models through editable external instructions. Revising several rules at once makes changes difficult to inspect, while judging each local edit only by its immediate score can discard useful intermediate states. We introduce AtomSearch, which keeps revision actions local while using subsequent skill states to guide search. Contrastive atomic editing links each primitive rule edit to failed and successful executions, a behavioral hypothesis, and an intended scope. Skill-State Tree Search (STS) evaluates short edit compositions and selectively retains them as executable states that can be revisited and extended. Outcome memory supplies later proposals with observed repair and regression statistics. We evaluate AtomSearch across four tasks and five target models. With deepseek-v4-flash, it exceeds SkillOpt on three of four tasks, including a 9.68-percentage-point gain on LiveMathematicianBench, and SkillOpt's final skills contain 2.3-19.7 times as many characters. In 67% of completed searches, the best validation state passes through an ancestor that was not highest-scoring at its depth. These results show how local rule edits can support the exploration of useful revision paths and yield compact skills, with additional offline evaluations used to explore alternative states."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e05214760d663efe78256371b09f2dab761b2cde", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19910", "CorpusId": 292903078}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e05214760d663efe78256371b09f2dab761b2cde", "title": "When Non-Adversarial Noise Hurts Learning: Benchmarking Skill Self-Evolution in Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19910, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2393075460", "name": "Tao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2408776350", "name": "Zhong-Tian Luo"}, {"authorId": "2337781398", "name": "Yu-Chen Long"}, {"authorId": "2510650780", "name": "Yi-Heng Lu"}, {"authorId": "2391722304", "name": "Yuxuan Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2498887897", "name": "Yan-Pei Gong"}, {"authorId": "2510746360", "name": "Si-Yi Cao"}, {"authorId": "2374185115", "name": "Yu-Qing Wu"}, {"authorId": "2408472091", "name": "Hao-Peng Jin"}, {"authorId": "2375780531", "name": "Zheng-Hao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2407027049", "name": "Yu-Jia Yang"}, {"authorId": "2507485315", "name": "Jing-Min Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2450306709", "name": "Yun-Yun Hou"}, {"authorId": "2362877110", "name": "Xin-Ming Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374110360", "name": "Hong-Zhu Yi"}, {"authorId": "2321411959", "name": "Jia-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510757623", "name": "Si-Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269471632", "name": "Nan Duan"}, {"authorId": "2399836822", "name": "Yan Huang"}, {"authorId": "2185177803", "name": "Liang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2367638723", "name": "Tian-Jin Huang"}, {"authorId": "2505476688", "name": "Yan-Yan Zou"}], "abstract": "Large language model agents increasingly improve by distilling experience into reusable skills, but errors in that experience can persist in learned rules and affect later tasks. Existing evaluations largely examine clean learning or perturbations encountered after skills are frozen, leaving robustness during self-evolution insufficiently understood. We introduce RSE-Bench, to our knowledge the first benchmark for evaluating skill self-evolution under ordinary, non-adversarial noise. It spans four domains and six benchmarks, with controlled perturbations to instructions, environmental evidence, execution records, and outcome--record pairings that preserve the underlying tasks and native scoring rules. The protocol separates noise during learning from input-side noise at evaluation, testing frozen skill stores on held-out tasks. We also introduce \\textsc{RSEClaw}, a method-agnostic framework supporting consistent noise injection and process diagnostics of actual exposure and skill updates. Across three methods and two backbones, noisy learning retains only $25.5\\%$--$42.1\\%$ of the aggregate gain achieved by clean self-evolution, depending on the noise type. Perturbations confined to execution records and outcome--record pairings cause average losses comparable to input-side noise, despite leaving the original executions and evaluations intact. Diagnostics further identify differences in candidate skill text that coexist with small changes in mean held-out performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "601729c66535041740dc2bd45d28259af02af196", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20312", "CorpusId": 292902096}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/601729c66535041740dc2bd45d28259af02af196", "title": "Reflect After Failure: Brain-Inspired Self-Evolving Agents for Embodied Spatial Intelligence", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20312, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510760228", "name": "Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2510750484", "name": "Xiao-Huan Gu"}, {"authorId": "2270303036", "name": "Yi-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2382924647", "name": "Shao-Bin Ling"}, {"authorId": "2510639356", "name": "Tian-Li Shi"}, {"authorId": "21735776", "name": "Jin-Gao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2277845056", "name": "Zhi-Meng Yin"}, {"authorId": "2344384666", "name": "Da-Peng Wu"}, {"authorId": "2375396939", "name": "Yun Wang"}], "abstract": "Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have made substantial progress in spatial reasoning, and self-evolving agents further extend these capabilities to embodied environments by learning from interactions. However, current self-evolution remains particularly challenging in embodied spatial tasks, complex and multi-step decisions from partial visual evidence making it difficult to determine why an execution fails. The failure may stem from misperception, insufficient exploration, or incorrect planning, yet execution feedback alone rarely reveals which component is responsible. As a result, simply accumulating interaction experience can reinforce ineffective strategies. To address this, we propose HiMeco, a brain-inspired self-evolving agent for embodied spatial intelligence, inspired by metacognitive monitoring and hippocampal memory consolidation. Through Hippocampal-Metacognitive Reflection, HiMeco diagnoses failures in perception-exploration-planning (PEP), then compares execution attempts to derive reusable strategies and guidance for avoiding recurring errors. It also abstracts interaction programs into parameterized skills, using evidence contracts and execution-based validation to determine their retention and reuse. These strategies and skills guide subsequent interactions, with memory updated only between rounds and model parameters kept frozen. HiMeco achieves state-of-the-art performance on ESI-Bench, and it also improves accuracy on EmbSpatial-Bench by 3.94 percentage points without evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c52aea8a4caad8e61e5bedc6e3f8c70d0afc3613", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20809", "CorpusId": 292981715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c52aea8a4caad8e61e5bedc6e3f8c70d0afc3613", "title": "A Self-Improving Agent that Grows its Own DSL: Oracle-Free Operator Induction for Exact Question Answering", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20809, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "1996191947", "name": "Yun-Shiuan Chuang"}, {"authorId": "2412748808", "name": "Yi-Rou Ge"}, {"authorId": "2346438807", "name": "Yi-Xi Li"}, {"authorId": "2511440848", "name": "Yi-Ming Bian"}, {"authorId": "2281612988", "name": "Zi-Jie Pan"}, {"authorId": "2511440173", "name": "Jenna L. Ballard"}, {"authorId": "2401264217", "name": "Uma Kona"}, {"authorId": "2309493012", "name": "Linsey Pang"}, {"authorId": "49036220", "name": "P. Mehrotra"}], "abstract": "A self-improving agent has to decide which new skills to keep, and in practice it keeps whatever earns a high reward, convinces a judge model, or compresses its library, even when the skill is wrong. That is fine until the answers have to be exactly right: over a table of transactions, a total is either right or wrong. We ask a harder question: can an agent grow what it can compute with no reward, no judge, and no answer key, and still be exactly right? A single frozen model runs two nested loops over a small, exact domain-specific language (DSL). The inner loop answers new questions only when two independently written expressions agree on every record. The outer loop discovers its own missing capability and authors an operator for it, admitted only when an independent second implementation agrees and the result obeys a conservation law, all without ground truth. The inner loop roughly triples the answerable-question set, and the outer loop self-extends on disjoint starting sets, inventing operators outside the starting closure; a no-induction control answers none of the new questions. The admission rule is empirically sound: it rejects 138 of 138 buggy operator variants under an independent reference, even one written by a different model family, where the admit-if-it-runs, self-consistency, and compression signals the field relies on admit most of them. Unmodified, the same mechanism self-extends on two further domains with unrelated schemas, business-ledger and clinical-encounter question answering, growing their answerable sets more than tenfold and nearly twentyfold. The loop transfers across model families too: run with further proposers, the gate stays sound and independent families self-extend comparably, with only the induction yield tracking each proposer's DSL fluency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1575f50952e54a61010c49d10e25b25d563189b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.21016", "CorpusId": 292982320}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1575f50952e54a61010c49d10e25b25d563189b4", "title": "CausalEvo: Causal Skill Self-Evolution for Health LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.21016, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509341066", "name": "Yu-Chen Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents for health can accumulate reusable textual skills from task feedback, such as calculation criteria, measurement time points, and formula units. Deciding which skills to retain is consequential: useful rules improve later answers, while faulty generalizations propagate errors. We propose CausalEvo, a method for causal skill self-evolution that estimates the relative effects of deliberately changed skill sets while holding the model weights, feedback cases, and rule count fixed. At each evolution step, a frozen LLM proposes candidate rules, which are pooled with the incumbent skills so that the current skill set remains a feasible choice. Paired skill replacements against shared background rules are scored on the same feedback cases; least squares converts the score differences into relative skill values, and the top-ranked rules are retained, favoring incumbents on ties. A decision-loss bound separates candidate support, approximation, and estimation errors. On six MedCalc-Bench-Verified tasks with 120 held-out cases, CausalEvo achieves 60.00% accuracy, compared with 55.83% for SkillOpt and 51.67% without skills. Across the recorded training configurations, which use different optimization budgets, it consumes 55.6% fewer training tokens than SkillOpt. Gains concentrate in particular calculations alongside regressions on others, and paired accuracy intervals include zero. CausalEvo turns task feedback into controlled decisions about skill retention and replacement. Project repository: https://github.com/txbrook/CausalEvo"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eecda8b2d1259f1602e6129a4833d007528616a9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20926", "CorpusId": 292981835}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eecda8b2d1259f1602e6129a4833d007528616a9", "title": "SkillNEO: Non-Elitist Evolutionary Optimization of Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20926, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2387928717", "name": "En-Ze Ma"}, {"authorId": "2344761288", "name": "Chun-Yu Miao"}, {"authorId": "2299292789", "name": "Yan-Kai Chen"}, {"authorId": "2364546424", "name": "Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2298840870", "name": "Lian-Cheng Fang"}, {"authorId": "2307008095", "name": "Ke-Ning Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2476469439", "name": "Han-Rong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2512006244", "name": "Mu-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2392624228", "name": "Xin-Le Yu"}, {"authorId": "2404547651", "name": "Cheng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2359100511", "name": "Wei-Chieh Huang"}, {"authorId": "2511848449", "name": "Zi-Wei Ren"}, {"authorId": "2243358440", "name": "Yun-Hao Du"}, {"authorId": "2268394001", "name": "Zhi-Xiang Shen"}, {"authorId": "2405783435", "name": "Xue Liu"}, {"authorId": "2375326707", "name": "Philip S. Yu"}, {"authorId": "2408873368", "name": "Zhen Wang"}], "abstract": "Agent skills turn procedural knowledge into reusable artifacts, allowing LLM agents to accumulate and refine expertise through experience. Building on this, skill evolution iteratively improves skills using execution feedback. However, improvement may require several interdependent edits, so low-reward versions can still be worth developing. Requiring immediate reward gains can discard useful partial repairs before their benefits emerge. Non-elitist evolution allows new versions to receive further edits despite scoring below earlier ones. For agent skills, this calls for evidence of what to preserve and repair next. We introduce SkillNEO (Non-Elitist Evolutionary Optimization for Agent Skills), which separately selects a version to use and versions to keep improving. Comparing how related versions perform the same task reveals working steps to preserve and remaining failures to fix. It can also revisit and revise earlier versions using later evidence. Across 86 SkillsBench tasks in eight domains, including software engineering and office work, SkillNEO raises GPT-5.4 Mini's average reward from 49.1% to 66.4%, within GPT-5.5 and GPT-5.6 Luna's original-skill range. Mini executes tasks, proposes revisions, and edits skills; final evaluation uses the same task instances. Gains span all eight domains. The same skills improve stronger models without further editing. On the 41 tasks with modified skills, reuse improves Luna by 10.5 percentage points and places GPT-5.5 10.6 points above its published original-skill reference. Execution records show these skills can benefit stronger models even when Mini still fails some task requirements. Search histories also show lower-scoring versions leading to skills ultimately selected for use. Together, these findings show that skill evolution can turn a weaker model's partial progress into reusable procedural knowledge for improving more capable agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8f83cef82e2c2e8a007ea34e9573dcde30a3209a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.20665", "CorpusId": 292982851}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8f83cef82e2c2e8a007ea34e9573dcde30a3209a", "title": "The World Won't Wait: Evaluating and Teaching Agents Under a Live Clock", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.20665, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2474919145", "name": "Ning-Xin Pan"}, {"authorId": "2447750407", "name": "Han-Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2352851777", "name": "Ye-Qing Shen"}, {"authorId": "2511445614", "name": "Ning Ding"}, {"authorId": "2408587961", "name": "Ye-Hui Tang"}, {"authorId": "2108232070", "name": "Wen-Tao Zhang"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents can solve complex interactive tasks, yet reasoning and tool calls take time. In continuously changing environments, an otherwise correct action may arrive too late. We introduce RealtimeBench, with 180 tasks across six game types, to evaluate agents while games continue running during inference. Paired evaluations show that GPT-6.1-sol outperforms the faster Jev when games are paused, but falls behind when they keep running. We propose Two-Timescale Offline Recursive Self-Improvement (TORSI): a slow teacher studies failures and tests corrections in paused games between attempts, then writes reusable instructions for a fast student. The student acts without teacher calls during gameplay, and validation scores determine which updates are adopted. Two rounds raise Jev's average test score from 13.51 to 24.19 on a 0--100 progress scale, improving all six game types. The instructions also improve average scores for three other models without retuning. These findings point toward real-time intelligence: enabling powerful LLM agents to remain effective in a continuously changing world by organizing deliberation, learning, and action across timescales."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.23726", "CorpusId": 293546508}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b16b80a648553146a8be8f184ef112a3cb0e17a6", "title": "RedDarwin: Evolving Jailbreak Skills against Frontier LLMs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.23726, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2458321075", "name": "Zhou-Yuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2374911264", "name": "Yu-Tao Wu"}, {"authorId": "1753845931", "name": "Hanxun Huang"}, {"authorId": "2328107098", "name": "Xiang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2340824747", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2387879011", "name": "Xing-Jun Ma"}], "abstract": "Red teaming must continually adapt as frontier LLM safeguards evolve, yet existing approaches largely search for adversarial prompts or optimize within fixed attack procedures. We observe that even when an attack becomes ineffective, its underlying mechanisms can remain useful and can be revised or recombined to construct stronger attacks. Based on this insight, we introduce RedDarwin, an agentic framework that shifts the unit of jailbreak evolution from prompts and fixed attacks to executable attack skills. RedDarwin consists of 47 reusable base skills and autonomously revises, composes, executes, and selects them using target-model feedback, allowing successful discoveries to become building blocks for subsequent evolution. RedDarwin produces 65 evolved skills, improving 44 of 47 base attacks and increasing their mean attack success rate (ASR) on AdvBench from 18.8% to 47.3%. These evolved skills consistently outperform baselines on the held-out StrongREJECT and frontier LLMs unseen during evolution; with only 10 target-model queries, RedDarwin achieves 100% ASR on GPT-5.6-Sol, 92% on Claude-Sonnet-5, and 80% on Gemini-3.8-Flash. Analysis of the evolution trajectories further shows that strong attacks often emerge from novel combinations of reusable mechanisms rather than from individually dominant ones. We believe this paradigm can accelerate adaptive red teaming and inform stronger safeguards, creating complementary value for both safety evaluation and defense."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8a9eb3f91cb1fb69854e840b2c7fd89a31e85d8b", "externalIds": {"DOI": "10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6", "CorpusId": 292843947}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8a9eb3f91cb1fb69854e840b2c7fd89a31e85d8b", "title": "Large language model multiagent systems for industrial problem discovery and prioritization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6.pdf", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1007/s44163-026-02468-6, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": null, "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "101894536", "name": "M. Adoul"}, {"authorId": "2161368912", "name": "Abdelhamid Ghoul"}], "abstract": "A persistent gap separates academic research priorities from the operational challenges faced by manufacturing firms. Established identification methods, among them Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) workshops, Lean audits and consultant engagements, are episodic, bounded by participant expertise and organised along departmental lines, which makes cross-departmental issues hard to surface. Multi-agent systems built on large language models (LLMs) have meanwhile shown collaborative problem-solving ability in software engineering and, more recently, in shopfloor scheduling and supply chain coordination. This article is a Perspective. It develops a framework and a research agenda; implementation and empirical evaluation fall outside its scope and are set out as its first research priorities. Three contributions are offered. The first is a synthesis of literature from industrial problem identification, LLM-based autonomous agents and manufacturing AI, which positions the proposal against existing industrial LLM agent work. The second is a framework in which six domain-specialised agents (production, quality, maintenance, supply chain, safety, energy) would collaborate through a five-phase protocol: worker-driven discovery, cross-departmental analysis, candidate-relationship detection, consensus-based financial assessment and debate-driven prioritisation. The prioritisation procedure is fully specified, including the normalisation of financial estimates, a measure of estimate dispersion, the derivation of criteria weights, a non-compensatory gate for safety-critical items and a mandatory human verification step. The third is a worked calculation on a hypothetical steel manufacturing vignette, included so that the procedure can be reproduced and scrutinised rather than as evidence of effectiveness. A preliminary qualitative readiness assessment and a three-horizon agenda of fourteen research questions complete the paper. The benefits identified are presented as hypotheses for that agenda to test rather than as demonstrated outcomes."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18813", "CorpusId": 292894673}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "title": "Self-Evolving Agent Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2174671074", "name": "Chu-Yang Wei"}, {"authorId": "2333357871", "name": "Hao-Xiang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2203918573", "name": "Ming-Hang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2434954462", "name": "Ken Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2367749242", "name": "Ji-Yan He"}, {"authorId": "46223995", "name": "Xi-Ren Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2468000469", "name": "Huan-Huan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2461908277", "name": "Yi-Fei Shen"}], "abstract": "Harness self-evolution revises an agent's software scaffolding, including prompts, memory, workflows, and executable components, while keeping model weights fixed. We formalize a feedback-driven process in which accepted revisions persist across runs. The framework separates update targets from proposal and admission mechanisms. It distinguishes changes to interpreted state from revisions to tools and code, repository-level control logic, and plugin composition. We characterize search through evolutionary optimization and examine its reliability through continual PAC-learning. Under explicit assumptions, the analysis yields an expected generalization bound, a retention bound conditional on historical regression budgets, and a high-probability admission rule using fresh, independent validation data. We examine how persistent harness revisions affect knowledge sharing and organizational workflows, with implications for scientific reliability, physical safety, and institutional accountability. Cases from prior work illustrate model--harness co-design, distributed execution, and plugin reuse. Reliable deployment requires continual validation as task distributions and evaluators change, with independent safeguards for learning, runtime privileges, and consequential actions. We provide a comprehensive 66-page discussion of related work, theory, algorithms, evaluation, applications, and the broader impact of Harness RSI, together with continuously updated code and supplementary materials, at https://github.com/zgcagi/Harness-RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c8a007b733acdd39b4d599a29c5aa436cc9223f0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17804", "CorpusId": 292894087}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8a007b733acdd39b4d599a29c5aa436cc9223f0", "title": "Learning from fracture: Teaching language models to design through physical experience", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17804, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Physics", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258719546", "name": "Bo Ni"}], "abstract": "How can language models learn to optimize physical designs through experience? We introduce FracLearn, an experience-abstraction-generalization framework evaluated through a deliberately unfamiliar, Tetris-inspired fracture game. The model first proposes architectures and examines simulated fracture responses to accumulate experience. Abstraction organizes these observations into conditional principles linking reinforcement placement, crack evolution and load transfer, with supporting evidence and failure conditions. Generalization challenges the model to combine these principles in new architectures and surpass the earlier design record. Campaigns demonstrate progressive refinement and recovery through hypothesis revision, yielding a best composite score 11.0% above the experience-stage record. Fracture analyses connect improved performance to crack deflection and temporary trapping as toughening mechanisms, alongside greater residual load capacity. Learning occurs through external knowledge and decisions, without model-weight updates. This approach offers a route to scientific agents that turn computational experiments into reusable physical knowledge for materials discovery and engineering design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "565fb1cf42ab53e862ddddbeed66ee2afa450a29", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18866", "CorpusId": 292895477}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/565fb1cf42ab53e862ddddbeed66ee2afa450a29", "title": "AutoAgent0: An Agentic Runtime for Safe Closed-Loop Driving", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18866, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2298930379", "name": "Jia-Geng Liu"}, {"authorId": "2316585215", "name": "Seth Z. Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2292466757", "name": "Ze-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "153652199", "name": "A. Huerta"}, {"authorId": "2510523474", "name": "Aidan Bayer-Calvert"}, {"authorId": "2377752532", "name": "Yu Bao"}, {"authorId": "2294514916", "name": "Hao-Tian Lin"}, {"authorId": "2510524546", "name": "Robert Spataru"}, {"authorId": "2364703176", "name": "Zi-Jun Cui"}, {"authorId": "2328344870", "name": "Yi-Ning Hong"}, {"authorId": "2316674209", "name": "Bo-Lei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "144303757", "name": "Jia-Qi Ma"}], "abstract": "Safe autonomous driving requires more than accurate planning at a single frame: it demands reliable closed-loop execution in dynamic environments. Although end-to-end driving policies have advanced rapidly, the accumulated closed-loop errors, especially in unseen scenarios, can push vehicles into states from which the same policy struggles to recover. We address the challenge by treating closed-loop driving safety as a test-time execution problem. We introduce AutoAgent0, an agentic runtime and harness framework that augments existing driving experts with complementary detection skills, rule-based action verification, and feedback-driven recovery. To address inaccurate scene perception, an independent detection module constructs scene context using complementary perception skills, and a rule-based verifier checks proposed trajectories against this context before execution. When the nominal trajectories are unreliable, the orchestrator of the runtime activates Recovery Planning, where an agentic loop constructs alternative action proposals from action primitives and iteratively refines them using verification feedback. This design combines independent scene assessment with feasibility checks and online recovery, addressing both perception errors and the limited recovery capabilities of individual experts. Evaluations on Fail2Drive and NavSafe-$\\infty$ demonstrate improved closed-loop robustness over single-expert execution in long-tail and safety-critical scenarios. These results demonstrate that runtime supervision can improve closed-loop robustness across multiple driving experts without retraining the underlying policies."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18792", "CorpusId": 292895945}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a4b43eb1827cf6d99800cdf42902eb74c61da74e", "title": "SEAR: Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18792, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509898711", "name": "Xin-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2261387782", "name": "Bang-Zheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2506680028", "name": "Han-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2504090520", "name": "Lin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2196998835", "name": "Andrea Yaoyun Cui"}, {"authorId": "2510651346", "name": "Zheng-Gang Tang"}, {"authorId": "8983218", "name": "S. Buch"}, {"authorId": "2510659666", "name": "De-Jia Xu"}], "abstract": "Frontier language-model agents can now improve robots on their own: they write control code, test it, and keep what works as skills and memories for the next task. We introduce SEAR (Self-Evolving Agentic Robotics), a framework and benchmark built around two questions. Is the agent really learning, or just trying more often? And does what it learns still help on new tasks, in a new simulator, and on a real robot? The framework runs an agent in three modes: it writes code that becomes the policy (code-as-policy), acts live in the control loop (agent-as-policy), or works as a researcher that fine-tunes a vision-language-action model (auto-research). The benchmark adds controls that separate retained experience from repeated attempts, familiar tasks, privileged simulator state, larger budgets, and luck. Across seven frontier language models, 330 simulated and five physical tasks, and over four years of agent runtime, we highlight three findings. First, the learning curve of a robotic agent depends both on the overall time budget and the task difficulty relative to model capability. Harder tasks make longer sessions worthwhile; on easier tasks, re-rolling short attempts does just as well. Second, control code and shared routines transfer across tasks and simulators, while task-specific solutions do not. On a real robot, we observe a performance gap between agent-designed practice and novel human-designed scenes. Third, different models evolve more effectively in different ways. Some do best acting live, others writing code first. And a text-only model keeps pace with a comparable multimodal one: perception helps with error assessment, but agentic reasoning and tool use can matter more. We release the benchmark and codebase to support future research."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4b0e27dd32e8db6654f45fe6436683acbbe97611", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17805", "CorpusId": 292896872}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4b0e27dd32e8db6654f45fe6436683acbbe97611", "title": "BRIDGE: Selective Boundary Repair with Dependency Graph Evidence in Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17805, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2352108190", "name": "En-Cheng Su"}, {"authorId": "2272217313", "name": "Peng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2495205559", "name": "Zi-Heng Hua"}, {"authorId": "2290397279", "name": "Qiu-Cheng Yu"}, {"authorId": "2308548523", "name": "Jian-Yu Wu"}, {"authorId": "2356376600", "name": "Chen Tang"}, {"authorId": "2202233953", "name": "Yi-Zhou Wang"}, {"authorId": "2210835156", "name": "Lin-Tao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2372191856", "name": "Jun-Jia Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2378070412", "name": "Xin-Zhu Ma"}, {"authorId": "2371415567", "name": "Xi Chen"}, {"authorId": "2362870789", "name": "Shi-Xiang Tang"}, {"authorId": "2319166044", "name": "Hou-Qiang Li"}, {"authorId": "2370955980", "name": "Ao-Ran Wang"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent collaboration has emerged as a critical paradigm for tackling complex, long-horizon tasks; however, while coordination protocols continue to advance, systemic failures at the inter-agent collaboration interface remain a pervasive bottleneck. In collaborative workflows, systems frequently suffer from \\emph{broken handoffs}---where critical task context is omitted, misrouted, or ignored despite each agent emitting locally plausible responses. To address this challenge, we present \\method, a verifiable runtime control framework that treats handoff repair as selective intervention over explicit dependency graphs. Central to \\method is an integrated fault-attribution and calibration pipeline, \\dataset, which decouples execution dependencies and injects counterfactual handoff perturbations to provide empirical supervision for runtime intervention. Leveraging this diagnostic foundation, \\method dynamically compiles observable interaction traces into a provenance graph to pinpoint unresolved dependencies, without accessing private agent states. It then evaluates candidate repairs through an empirical gate that weighs expected gains against disruption risks, and executes repairs only when formal local postconditions hold. Extensive evaluations across demanding benchmarks demonstrate that \\method boosts task success by up to 12.3 absolute percentage points on SILO-BENCH and 11.1 on TeamBench. Across both backbones, \\method maintains strict cost-effectiveness, adding as little as 2.0\\% marginal token overhead. Furthermore, on 562 handoff-relevant traces from the MAST benchmark, \\method achieves 69.8\\% F1 in a single pass, establishing a reliable operational boundary for verifiable coordination repair."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "914f15e6585cb4169373f3b891a6159bccde065e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17740", "CorpusId": 292897127}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/914f15e6585cb4169373f3b891a6159bccde065e", "title": "Coupled Harness--Model Evolution for Scientific Equation Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17740, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2272217313", "name": "Peng-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2355335469", "name": "Jia-Quan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2352108190", "name": "En-Cheng Su"}, {"authorId": "2323315765", "name": "Yun-Bo Long"}, {"authorId": "2321477871", "name": "Zi-Hang Zeng"}, {"authorId": "2510655972", "name": "Xin-Ping Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510651534", "name": "Han Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2367176905", "name": "Jun-Chi Yu"}, {"authorId": "2362870789", "name": "Shi-Xiang Tang"}, {"authorId": "2042692068", "name": "Aoran Wang"}, {"authorId": "2371415567", "name": "Xi Chen"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly used to discover scientific equations from experimental data. Such a discovery system has two parts that must work together: a library of executable computational tools and the model's tool-use policy. The tools supply the solving capability, from diagnosing structure in the data to fitting candidate equations; the policy plans and orchestrates their use. Yet existing systems adapt only one part, keeping either the toolset or the model fixed, which leaves the corresponding failures uncorrectable. We develop a coupled evolution framework that improves both parts from the same verifiable fit-error signal. In the tool-evolution phase, an agent grows an executable tool library from a small set of generic numerical operations by diagnosing its own failures: it authors a new tool when a capability is missing and revises its selection rules when the right tool went unused. In the policy-evolution phase, a small open model (roughly 3B active parameters) is fine-tuned on successful tool-use trajectories collected with the frozen library, on training formulas screened to be disjoint from all evaluation benchmarks. Ablations show the two phases are complementary: with no tool library at all the same model solves almost nothing, the tools account for most of the gain, and trajectory training adds a further gain that is largest on the most difficult problems. The full system solves 79.1/71.3\\% of LSR-Synth problems at fit tolerances $10^{-2}$/$10^{-3}$, reaches 63.8\\% pooled accuracy on the distributionally different SRSD-Feynman panel, and obtains a normalized CP3-28 aggregate of $-7.27$. These results suggest a broader recipe for scientific agents: let the model and its instruments improve each other under verifiable feedback."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eb97fb659c7a5619d9d3d8ad9da25c1ea14d6fd9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17860", "CorpusId": 292895876}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb97fb659c7a5619d9d3d8ad9da25c1ea14d6fd9", "title": "Socratic Skill Learning: Interleaving Teaching and Practice for Transferable Agent Skills", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17860, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2312779599", "name": "Le-Tian Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2384921703", "name": "Han Li"}, {"authorId": "2269827594", "name": "Yi-Fan Yao"}, {"authorId": "2384335797", "name": "Xin-Ping Lei"}, {"authorId": "2408464177", "name": "Xin-Yu Che"}, {"authorId": "2510545379", "name": "Jia-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2336523218", "name": "Dian-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510519566", "name": "Yu-Cheng Fu"}, {"authorId": "2279661445", "name": "Jia-Kai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2279540935", "name": "Zhi-Qi Bai"}, {"authorId": "2279560018", "name": "Wen-Bo Su"}, {"authorId": "2346051109", "name": "Jia-Heng Liu"}], "abstract": "Agent Skills improve LLM agents by making task knowledge reusable across sessions. Learning transferable Skills requires evidence about a procedure and its conditions of use, which a successful task attempt can leave untested. We introduce Socratic Skill Learning (SSL), which interleaves expert teaching with Student practice. An Educator guides investigation while the Student remains the sole environment operator and Skill author, able to question, test, and adapt advice. To test reuse beyond the learning task, we construct SkillTransferBench, with 35 task families and 408 executable cases, pairing public variation specifications with held-out evaluation in fresh solver sessions. On the main 22-family, 155-case cohort, Fixed-Window SSL reaches 0.801 utility, versus 0.723 without a Skill, 0.761 for adapted Trace2Skill (trajectory distillation), and 0.751 for adapted SKILL-KD (contrastive distillation). Across three model pairs on shared families, interleaved guidance outperforms expert authoring and post-hoc teaching. Process analysis links guidance to Student writing and package revision; a case study shows the Student testing and refining a proposed procedure for later reuse. As demand for new lessons falls, the cost of fixed-window review grows. Issue-Guided SSL tracks unresolved questions across Skill revisions to select and focus review, reaching 0.805 utility with 61.7% fewer Educator tokens and 16.9% fewer total acquisition tokens than Fixed-Window SSL on the main cohort."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1d18b1ddd6c27d192a2cbb16d3523ab6e0954d73", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18216", "CorpusId": 292897577}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1d18b1ddd6c27d192a2cbb16d3523ab6e0954d73", "title": "TextualRL: Textual Reinforcement Learning for Context Management", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18216, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2231570984", "name": "Tianyu Huai"}, {"authorId": "2280186807", "name": "Jie Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2322951730", "name": "Bo Mao"}, {"authorId": "2256591738", "name": "Qin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2384802294", "name": "Liang He"}, {"authorId": "2361484236", "name": "Xi-Peng Qiu"}], "abstract": "LLM agents can adapt to task feedback by revising the textual context that guides their behavior, without changing their model parameters. However, turning execution rewards into reusable context updates is challenging: individual trajectories offer limited comparative evidence about what to change, while task-specific fixes may overgeneralize when incorporated into a shared context. We introduce TextualRL, a gradient-free reinforcement learning framework that treats a group of rollouts from the same task under the same context, rather than an isolated trajectory, as the unit of policy critique. Groupwise Policy Critique contrasts successful and failed rollouts within a task; when outcomes are uniform, it draws on cross-task evidence to identify behaviors worth preserving or formulate repair hypotheses. Cross-Group Policy Update reviews the resulting proposals against evidence pooled across groups, including observations that yield no proposed edit, to refine their content and applicability. Reviewed proposals form a bounded textual patch, with adoption of the candidate context determined by held-out validation rewards. Together, these components establish a reward-driven policy optimization loop in which multiple attempts jointly inform reusable context updates. We evaluate TextualRL with five target agents on six benchmarks. Averaged over the six benchmarks, TextualRL improves test accuracy over the base model by 24.4 and 15.3 percentage points for GPT-5.5 and Qwen3.8-27B, respectively."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cc768d806622292a5ab5badf69ab4c0b974973f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18867", "CorpusId": 292903701}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cc768d806622292a5ab5badf69ab4c0b974973f", "title": "EmbodiedEvo: Self-Evolving Coding Agent For Physical Intelligence", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18867, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291312857", "name": "Wen-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2221943418", "name": "Yi-Zhe Chi"}, {"authorId": "2537322", "name": "You-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2390406207", "name": "Chun-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2458702621", "name": "Xiao-Qiu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2291442009", "name": "Ming-Ju Gao"}, {"authorId": "2446282189", "name": "Zheng Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2410898209", "name": "De-Yao Hong"}, {"authorId": "143940914", "name": "C. Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2383097217", "name": "Kai-Sen Yang"}, {"authorId": "2429465105", "name": "Qin-Huai Na"}], "abstract": "Coding agents can construct executable programs and revise them through feedback, offering a foundation for embodied control without target-task fine-tuning. Through a programmatic robot interface, the agent observes the scene, executes control programs, and adapts them to the resulting physical state, serving as the closed-loop policy. However, this within-episode adaptation does not automatically preserve experience across scenes. Our trajectory analysis shows that agents spend interaction and inference budgets establishing effective operations and identifying their preconditions, while fresh agents cannot reuse these discoveries. This motivates EmbodiedEvo, a self-evolving coding agent that distills physical feedback into reusable code Skills and diagnostic Lessons. Experience is acquired on one Playground scene per task, then frozen for independent agents in unseen scenes, without updating model weights. On RoboDojo-Sim, EmbodiedEvo achieves an average score of 44.48 and a success rate of 37.82\\%, surpassing all officially verified results. Paired tests show the largest gains on memory-dependent tasks and successful reuse of coordinated procedures, while fine contact control remains difficult. Experience from stronger models also improves weaker executors: Astra's experience raises Sol and Luna's success rates by 8.86 and 5.50 percentage points over no experience. Compared with raw interaction history, Skills and Lessons achieve comparable task performance at 53.0\\% lower execution cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b28fa5f5f452859abb91ddea744e12e61eab1939", "externalIds": {"PubMedCentral": "13658227", "DOI": "10.1038/s44386-026-00075-0", "CorpusId": 292833136, "PubMed": "42864384"}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b28fa5f5f452859abb91ddea744e12e61eab1939", "title": "The rise of agentic AI in drug discovery from static tools to autonomous virtual scientists", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s44386-026-00075-0.pdf", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBYNCND", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13658227, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Medicine"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2377435411", "name": "Dong-Hai Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2310703435", "name": "Mei-Jing Fang"}, {"authorId": "2509985660", "name": "Le-Xiao Mao"}, {"authorId": "2307563764", "name": "Yu-Chen Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2072402373", "name": "Qun Su"}, {"authorId": "2149132903", "name": "Hui Shi"}, {"authorId": "2313887077", "name": "Hong-Xia Xu"}, {"authorId": "2284831457", "name": "Lan-Qing Li"}, {"authorId": "2337540728", "name": "Ting-Jun Hou"}, {"authorId": "2434922853", "name": "Chang-Yu Hsieh"}], "abstract": "Driven by large language models, AI agents are transforming drug discovery, enabling AI to break free from task-specific tools and advance toward the vision of autonomous virtual scientists. This perspective reviews applications across the R&D lifecycle. To evaluate system maturity, we introduce a 6-level \u201cSpectrum of Agentic Autonomy.\u201d We advocate for novel evaluation paradigms centered on full-chain traceability and outline adoption barriers, positioning AI agents as pragmatic scientist-in-the-loop co-pilots."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4233542406288f7c83c271541246d6b6380ed05b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17130", "CorpusId": 292895429}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4233542406288f7c83c271541246d6b6380ed05b", "title": "EvoQuantLab: Training-Free Dual-Loop Evolution for Auditable Quantitative Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17130, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2505831422", "name": "Hao-Lin Yang"}, {"authorId": "2510268568", "name": "Zhi-Qiang Qian"}, {"authorId": "2510652303", "name": "Chu-Tian Chen"}, {"authorId": "2339983780", "name": "Ji-Peng Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2359285644", "name": "Zheng-Hao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2496847843", "name": "Tang-Jian-Guo Deng"}, {"authorId": "2509359983", "name": "Jian Xie"}, {"authorId": "2058167090", "name": "Shuai-Shuai Gong"}, {"authorId": "2403462291", "name": "Yi-Ke Guo"}, {"authorId": "2350527271", "name": "Si-Rui Han"}], "abstract": "Automated alpha factor discovery is critical for modern quantitative research. While large language models (LLMs) have increasingly been integrated into quantitative workflows, existing agent-based approaches remain highly susceptible to severe backtest overfitting. To address this challenge, we propose EvoQuantLab, a training-free, dual-loop self-evolving framework for robust factor discovery. Instead of relying entirely on LLM generation, EvoQuantLab deploys an inner genetic-programming (GP) loop that dramatically improves token efficiency while evolving typed factor expressions under structural and diversity constraints. An outer diagnosis loop then systematically audits evidence across cycles to drive protocol-level improvements. Furthermore, our evaluation pipeline applies multi-metric validation to rigorously filter out spurious factors before deployment. Extensive out-of-sample experiments across both daily and hourly frequencies -- covering equities and cryptocurrency -- demonstrate the robustness of EvoQuantLab. Specifically, on daily CSI 500 and S&P 500 tasks, EvoQuantLab achieves leading annualized returns of 18.34% and 21.30%, consistently outperforming existing agent baselines. On high-turnover hourly cryptocurrency data, EvoQuantLab delivers a 37.91% annualized return while effectively avoiding execution collapse."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "885b6a59756ed7b78baed3d546c80fd3ac05a8f7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16776", "CorpusId": 292894596}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/885b6a59756ed7b78baed3d546c80fd3ac05a8f7", "title": "Game-Creation-Agents: Autonomous Game Production", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16776, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2482966228", "name": "T.-R. Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2453837409", "name": "Jia-Qi Tang"}, {"authorId": "2499344877", "name": "Kenney Ma"}, {"authorId": "2446286742", "name": "Wen-Hao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2283873071", "name": "Jiarui Yao"}, {"authorId": "2382762486", "name": "Bo-Ye Niu"}, {"authorId": "2510552124", "name": "Hai-Yang Guo"}, {"authorId": "2452176756", "name": "Yi-Fei Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2268465257", "name": "Huan-Jin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2329739061", "name": "Pu Jian"}, {"authorId": "2356100198", "name": "Rui-Huang Li"}, {"authorId": "2510695941", "name": "Hai-Tao Wu"}, {"authorId": "2268626103", "name": "Yu-Shi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2355902163", "name": "Xiang-Yan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2336094956", "name": "Y. Sun"}, {"authorId": "2298147619", "name": "Kai-Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2348451895", "name": "Tian-Yu Pang"}], "abstract": "Game-Creation-Agents (GCA) turns a natural-language request into a runnable Phaser 4 browser game. The pipeline first builds a multimodal game design document that couples mechanics and scene contracts to grounded visual references. It then projects that document into a runtime scaffold, machine-readable contracts, and deterministic tests. Eight specialized agents next carry out production: scenes are implemented serially, assets are produced in parallel, and one reduced manifest joins the two branches into a single build. Build-linked real-UI checks and a code-blind critic then verify that build and decide whether the game may ship. Each blocker returns to the agent that owns the affected artifact for a bounded repair, and the game is released once all the gates pass. We evaluate finished games separately, through their rendered interfaces alone, over prompt-matched tasks run under a common protocol. GCA achieves stronger requirement satisfaction, core mechanics, content depth, and functional visuals than a comparable open pipeline. In a side-by-side human study, raters also score GCA's games higher on every dimension and prefer them overall."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3861b358f883f9a3c176ceaa2d1e6147b2d4175d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17488", "CorpusId": 292895830}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3861b358f883f9a3c176ceaa2d1e6147b2d4175d", "title": "Origin Attribution for Unsafe Behavior in Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Coding Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17488, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509453493", "name": "Han Yu"}, {"authorId": "2447310122", "name": "Jia-Ming Cui"}], "abstract": "As language-model-based coding agents evolve their programs and reuse code from one another, safety failures can acquire a history that extends beyond the agent in which they are detected. An unsafe change may originate in one agent, propagate through intermediate recipients, and survive subsequent revisions before an audit reveals it. Understanding this history is important for identifying the introducing change and distinguishing inherited behavior from independently occurring failures. We study origin attribution in self-evolving multi-agent coding systems: recovering the agent and program version that introduced an observed unsafe behavior into the target's code lineage, or determining that it arose locally. This task is challenging because rewriting obscures textual similarity, independent occurrences confound temporal ordering, and intermediate recipients can resemble the source. We propose a method that combines behavioral fingerprint matching with historical localization and recursive tracing. It compares candidate implementations beyond the unsafe outcome, retains evidence from multiple historical versions, and follows matching candidates upstream. On 158 queries across 85 controlled scenarios, the method achieves 96.8% exact attribution accuracy, compared with 77.2% for exhaustive fingerprint matching without recursion. Component ablations and evaluation on 34 additional queries using the same method and hyperparameters demonstrate the complementary roles of multiple representatives and recursive tracing. Further checks extend the evaluation to approval-boundary and resource-owner errors. Together, these results show how behavioral and historical evidence can recover the origins of unsafe behavior through code revision and cross-agent propagation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17370", "CorpusId": 292896173}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "title": "HarnessTTT: Learn to Optimize Harness at Test-Time for Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17370, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2269426296", "name": "Ying-Zi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2146176464", "name": "Yu-Long Cao"}, {"authorId": "49088449", "name": "Nanxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2359179354", "name": "Fang Wu"}, {"authorId": "48378494", "name": "Yi-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2347006409", "name": "James Y. Zou"}, {"authorId": "2382829310", "name": "Ye-Jin Choi"}, {"authorId": "2273519476", "name": "Chao-Wei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Scientific discovery with language models requires finding the best valid solution within a finite computational budget. We study how feedback from this search can improve the procedure used to conduct it. We introduce HarnessTTT, a test-time learning framework that trains a harness proposer while keeping the executor frozen. The proposer generates executable harnesses comprising system prompts, reusable skills, tools, middleware, and execution controls. Each harness guides a budgeted discovery rollout, whose outcome provides feedback for updating the proposer. A discovery-oriented policy-gradient objective combines gap-normalized rewards, leave-one-out advantages (RLOO), and max-sharpening to favor high-performing proposals. Generated components are validated before execution, and the search retains the best valid solution and selected harnesses across rounds. Across fourteen discovery tasks in mathematics, systems, GPU kernels, heuristic programming, astrodynamics, and biology, HarnessTTT with a frozen 9B executor improves on OpenEvolve with the same executor on all eleven benchmark tasks (e.g., from 1.17 to 2.64 on circle packing), improves on the discovery-fine-tuned Finch-9B by up to 92%, holds the best open-weight sub-10B result on nine, and reaches 0.380919 on Erd\\H{o}s minimum overlap, better than the best human reference of 0.380927. Scaling the proposer and frozen executor to 27B improves on eleven of fourteen tasks, and transferring the learned harnesses to GPT-5.5 matches or exceeds the strongest reported system of any backbone on nine of fourteen tasks. Together, these results show that harness search can itself be learned at test time: a trained proposer lets a small frozen executor search effectively, and the harnesses it finds carry over to stronger executors."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4985ffe9cbd8f54d20c5ef4b133817d51550e194", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16129", "CorpusId": 292897020}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4985ffe9cbd8f54d20c5ef4b133817d51550e194", "title": "PID-Gated Continual Prompt Adaptation Under Noisy Feedback", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16129, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2298963010", "name": "Zhentao Xu"}], "abstract": "Deploying LLM agents in non-stationary environments requires continually revising their instructions as the task drifts, yet rewriting after every reported mistake can turn noisy feedback into persistent errors. This raises an often-overlooked scheduling question in prompt adaptation: when should feedback trigger a lasting instruction update, and at what editor-call cost? Taking inspiration from proportional-integral-derivative (PID) control, we introduce a family of lightweight gates that react to the current error, accumulate evidence over time, and roll back deterioration after an edit. We benchmark these gates against both simple baselines and advanced prompt optimizers (TextGrad and GEPA), over a diverse suite of drifting tasks, LLM parameter sizes, feedback-noise levels, and random seeds. PID-gated continual prompt adaptation yields a more favorable Pareto trade-off between task performance (high accuracy, low error) and editor-call budget than the baselines considered, pointing to control-theoretic scheduling as a simple, general tool for robust prompt adaptation under unreliable feedback."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "885124cfca7196d2e4643cadbb0e18230760dcaf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16125", "CorpusId": 292896193}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/885124cfca7196d2e4643cadbb0e18230760dcaf", "title": "Imitation Holds a Key to Evolution: Localized Repair and Verified Skill Acquisition for Adaptive Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16125, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510560656", "name": "Kai Hu"}, {"authorId": "2344121138", "name": "Hua Huang"}], "abstract": "An agent can retain successful trajectories without learning which part of an experience should repair a new discrepancy. We formulate passive imitation (PI) as an architectural loop that localizes a violated contract, extracts a local goal, proposes an effect-matched skill, tests a small patch, and retains evidence after full-task verification. We instantiate this loop as a deterministic symbolic controller and analyze its finite repair-query complexity under explicit identifiability and noninterference assumptions. Across ten simulated tool worlds, PI solves all 600 cold held-out composition tasks with three allowed small trials, compared with 6.17% for a whole-trajectory control, while an equally expressive compositional repair control matches PI. On an unseen tool revision, PI achieves 70.0% success versus 24.0% without small trials and 79.0% with self-trial enumeration; an exploratory 24-task adaptation stream subsequently raises first-submission success to 100%, supporting a limited correction-and-retention mechanism rather than a unique incentive advantage or general intelligence. The CPU-only implementation, integrity checks, and reproducible results are available at https://github.com/FriendOfYishan/PI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e1df110e6e90ee701dc587883abef6d4ff3b84ca", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17114", "CorpusId": 292897002}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e1df110e6e90ee701dc587883abef6d4ff3b84ca", "title": "D-World: An Explicit World-Language-Action Model with Recursive Self-Improvement under Dual Control", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17114, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2503633209", "name": "Wen-Kai Xuan"}, {"authorId": "2510655097", "name": "Bai-Chao Wang"}], "abstract": "Frontier large language models (LLMs) now reason about space well enough to program robots, yet the world models behind robot policies are implicit, held in network weights or predicted video, and cannot be inspected or corrected directly. We present D-World, an explicit world-language-action model built on the Sense, Plan and Act decomposition. A perception agent writes the scene as an executable digital twin using generated vision and geometry code. A planning agent searches for actions in simulation and, after each executed action chunk, revises the twin's object states and named physical parameters against what the robot measured. A parameter revision is accepted only if replaying the latest chunk does not increase any measured residual beyond its tolerance, and every chunk is checked in the updated twin before it runs. Structural mismatches return to perception for reconstruction. We call this measurement-grounded cycle of correction and revision recursive self-improvement (RSI), in which the LLM stays frozen while the world it acts in improves. We model the choice of actions that both advance the task and expose model error as a dual control problem and show that the cost of incorporating evidence into the twin sets the threshold at which probing pays, so cheap local revision counteracts the turn-off of cautious controllers. Each episode runs a real-to-sim-to-real loop, and its recorded twins can be refitted and re-rendered offline into higher-fidelity environments for post-training. Because perception and planning communicate only through the twin and language, either can be replaced by a learned model, and since inference dominates the loop, the loop speeds up with faster models. On a dual-arm robot, towel and toaster tasks show local revisions, such as one reducing the bread's rendered-depth residual from 22.4 to 1.5 mm, that let execution continue without reconstruction."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d09e2d5a60e8715e199ef1f72a12c0851caf023a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33029", "CorpusId": 294227916}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d09e2d5a60e8715e199ef1f72a12c0851caf023a", "title": "Lessons from Luck: Do LLM-Written Lessons Carry Chance into an Agent's Next Decision?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33029, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "67134007", "name": "Kazuki Nakayashiki"}], "abstract": "Self-improving agents write lessons from the outcomes of their runs; when outcomes are noisy, a lesson can carry chance into later decisions. We measure one link of this chain in a registered study. In 256 synthetic worlds, each a recurring task with two interchangeable tools of recorded reliability, a scripted previous run used the more reliable tool (a good decision) or the less reliable one (a bad decision), with an assigned outcome: success or a transient failure. A model wrote a lesson from the run with ReasoningBank's memory-writing instruction; a fresh session of the same model, holding the authoritative log and the previous result, chose the next run's first tool with one memory item: that lesson, the lesson from the other outcome, an irrelevant item, or an unreasoned directive. The protocol was frozen, deposited and timestamped before any confirmatory call; 11 LLM endpoints gave 90,084 answers. After a chance failure of the better tool, the panel's lessons carried the chance (registered reading luck-carrying; interaction +0.112, 95% interval [+0.097, +0.126]): readers named the worse tool in 11.6% of next runs with the lesson written from that failure, against 0.4% with an irrelevant item and 0.4% with the lesson written from a success. The effect was +0.042 where the log stated that failures are independent and +0.181 where it did not (descriptive). After a lucky success of the worse tool, the interaction was bounded below 0.10 (+0.002), a bound, not evidence of no harm. In exploratory readings, 6 endpoints carried the chance (up to +0.352); 5 were bounded below 0.10. In secondary comparisons, a three-sentence decision-quality note in the writing instruction lowered the worse-tool rate after a chance failure to 7.5%, and one instruction for both outcomes with a no-change option to 3.1%; neither met the registered harm-reduction rule at every such endpoint."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8c81a9d8b5e524d7e6bb644e20e9f4d98fdbea50", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15814", "CorpusId": 292894097}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8c81a9d8b5e524d7e6bb644e20e9f4d98fdbea50", "title": "ReTrace: Cross-Stage Failure Attribution for Self-Improving Robotic Manipulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15814, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350014783", "name": "Yue Yu"}, {"authorId": "2289341222", "name": "Bin Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2487104716", "name": "Jun-Hui Li"}, {"authorId": "2334444592", "name": "Jia-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2194748419", "name": "Shi-Jie Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2108536365", "name": "Jing-Jing Chen"}], "abstract": "General-purpose multimodal foundation models are increasingly capable of directly controlling robots, yet improving their manipulation capabilities through autonomous interaction remains challenging. A key challenge to such self-improvement is learning from failures in complex manipulation tasks involving cross-stage dependencies, where an action may succeed locally but cause a later stage to fail. To address this challenge, we introduce ReTrace, a self-improvement framework for robotic manipulation through autonomous physical interaction and retrospective reasoning, without provided demonstrations or model-parameter updates. ReTrace retrospectively traces downstream failures to earlier decisions, proposes targeted corrections, and evaluates them through subsequent interactions. Across attempts, ReTrace progressively consolidates validated corrections into a reusable manipulation skill that guide subsequent execution. We evaluate ReTrace on four real-world manipulation tasks using a Franka Research 3 robot. On the two tasks with strong cross-stage dependencies, GPT-6 Astra-low improves from 0% to 80% success on plug insertion and from 0% to 100% on ball pouring, while GPT-6 Sol-xhigh improves from 0% to 10% and 0% to 90%, respectively. The learned skills also transfer across model and reasoning configurations and reduce inference-time decision cost in most evaluated settings."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15915", "CorpusId": 292894504}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "title": "VIEWCRAFT: Evolving What Multimodal Agents See and How They Read It", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15915, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2375625516", "name": "Bin-Xiao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2407465447", "name": "Rui-Chuan An"}, {"authorId": "2269737481", "name": "Bocheng Zou"}, {"authorId": "2214830561", "name": "Weihua Du"}, {"authorId": "2276843415", "name": "Xin-Yu Wei"}, {"authorId": "2405115995", "name": "Hang-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2510650275", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2147420753", "name": "Ren-Ye Yan"}, {"authorId": "2326302007", "name": "Chengqi Duan"}, {"authorId": "2331326371", "name": "Hang Hua"}], "abstract": "Reusable skills improve multimodal agents without updating model weights, but their effectiveness depends on both the evidence presented and the procedure used to read it. We introduce ViewCraft, which jointly evolves an executable evidence interface and its reading procedure. The interface selects, transforms, and organizes observations; the reader interprets the resulting views. Failure traces guide revisions, replay and paired evaluation retain useful changes, and interface projection recovers visual edits from eligible rejected joint proposals. Across eight benchmarks, ViewCraft improves over direct execution by an average of $6.1$ points in task score, with gains on every benchmark. The advantage persists at a matched input-token budget. On all five multi-source tasks, the learned interface uses 8%-15% fewer input tokens than Initial skill. The learned skills also transfer across executors and outperform direct execution in every evaluated transfer, supporting evidence construction and reading as a coupled, reusable skill."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "309e6eaa9b79b0dcc2f4451cdc507c2848108c58", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15481", "CorpusId": 292894198}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/309e6eaa9b79b0dcc2f4451cdc507c2848108c58", "title": "SkillFusion: Towards Reliable Multi-Skill Composition for LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15481, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362086277", "name": "En-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "2216308108", "name": "Xinye Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2300335251", "name": "Yi-Ming Li"}, {"authorId": "2311518604", "name": "Bing-Run Yang"}, {"authorId": "2510516674", "name": "Nan-Jun Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2383937215", "name": "Chun-Shu Xia"}, {"authorId": "2028819641", "name": "H. Yao"}, {"authorId": "2386950242", "name": "Zhan Qin"}], "abstract": "Independently authored skills can impose incompatible requirements, competing preferences, and redundant instructions when jointly loaded by an LLM agent. We propose SkillFusion, an offline compiler that reconciles these interactions into a reusable merged skill. Its central idea is to make composition decisions explicit through typed, scoped constraints linked to their source directives. A type-scope gate selects candidate interactions, while selective fallback revisits pairs potentially missed by structural pruning. A classifier identifies interaction types, and an adversarial verifier re-examines uncertain judgments. The resolver applies conditional routing or explicit priorities to hard conflicts, verified abstraction to soft conflicts, and deduplication to redundant instructions. Source-linked checks guide synthesis and compression, and metadata synthesis supports discovery of the merged skill. The resulting artifact retains associated scripts and resources and can be reused across tasks and executor models. SkillFusion achieves the highest mean Success among evaluated non-oracle methods on all three agent benchmarks and on both transfer benchmarks across three executor models. At the largest tested instruction configurations, it achieves over 2.5 times the Success of unresolved multi-skill loading, highlighting the growing value of reconciliation as composition becomes more demanding. The code will be open-sourced after the review process concludes."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2ff16251a6299fbb84763f3144ed028298fe3a32", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15714", "CorpusId": 292894808}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2ff16251a6299fbb84763f3144ed028298fe3a32", "title": "MedHarnessBench: A Unified Benchmark for Healthcare Agent Evaluation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15714, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510269197", "name": "Rui Tang"}, {"authorId": "2253847124", "name": "Hong-Qiu Wang"}], "abstract": "Healthcare agents must transform longitudinal clinical evidence into reliable actions through heterogeneous tools and stateful workflows, conditions under which model-centric systems may lose context, misuse tools, fail to verify consequential transitions, or cross authorization boundaries. Existing medical-agent benchmarks primarily emphasize terminal task outcomes, making it difficult to determine whether execution failures arise from the agent model, the runtime harness, or their interaction. We present the Medical Harness Protocol, an outcome-preserving framework that independently evaluates seven healthcare-specific process dimensions: Care Action Fulfillment, Clinical Resource Competence, Patient-Evidence Alignment, Clinical Workflow Continuity, Clinical Auditability, Closed-Loop Verification, and Clinical Conduct Boundaries. A normalized intervention trace further tracks whether runtime support is available, activated, delivered, adopted by the agent, and propagated into subsequent behavior. We instantiate the protocol as MedHarnessBench, comprising 342 executable tasks across FHIR/EHR operations, healthcare-administration interfaces, and multimodal clinical tools. Across more than 16,000 controlled task executions, MedHarness yields broad improvements in terminal task success, intermediate subtask completion, and healthcare-specific process quality. We further evaluate MedHarness against multiple general-purpose harnesses and use controlled single-fault perturbations to verify the sensitivity and discrimination of the seven-dimensional evaluation. These results demonstrate that healthcare-specific runtime support can substantially improve both task completion and execution quality, while revealing failures that terminal outcomes alone cannot capture. Code and resources are available at https://github.com/Tomry1114/Awesome-Agent-Harness-for-Healthcare."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f71c66db8253a8b55a201fcfda12ed2907c5477d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15244", "CorpusId": 292894745}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f71c66db8253a8b55a201fcfda12ed2907c5477d", "title": "EvolvAttack: Backdooring self Evolving VLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15244, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2451538809", "name": "Chuan Zou"}, {"authorId": "2383112342", "name": "Yang Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2109967407", "name": "Zexin Li"}, {"authorId": "2363584140", "name": "Xiao-Nan Luo"}, {"authorId": "2347695427", "name": "Si-Min Chen"}], "abstract": "Self-evolving mechanisms let vision-language model (VLM) agents continually adapt through evolving memory, but they also allow malicious behavior to emerge only after deployment. We introduce a new attack surface, the self-evolving backdoor, whose payload stays dormant in the initial agent and is progressively activated as the agent evolves. Unlike conventional backdoors, which can be exposed by testing triggered inputs on a static model, our attack requires both a visual trigger and a sufficiently evolved memory state; static scanning with clean memory thus yields near-zero attack success and may deem the agent benign. We realize this through memory-aware backdoor training, which couples trigger activation with the agent's evolving memory while preserving benign behavior unless the trigger co-occurs with payload-related memory. Across three VLM backbones, four agent pipelines, and recency-, semantic-, and reward-based memory evolution, the attack preserves normal functionality and stays nearly inactive before evolution (0.5% attack success rate), yet exceeds 95% attack success within 100 interactions, revealing a stealthy threat unique to self-evolving agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15964", "CorpusId": 292895466}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f7f151d0c7b964c558e07fabeb5728a9270dc71c", "title": "Small but Mighty: Test Suites as Improvement Signals for Self-Evolving Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15964, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2249879747", "name": "Wei Cheng"}, {"authorId": "3007026", "name": "Wen-Chao Yu"}, {"authorId": "2313576252", "name": "Da-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2249915042", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}], "abstract": "A self-evolving agent rewrites its own harness and must decide which rewrites to keep, so its improvement signal is the check a rewrite has to pass. Verifying a rewrite by agent rollout is expensive and subjective, while executable checks such as the repository's own tests are too sparse to catch behavior-changing rewrites, rejecting only a quarter of them. We instead take test feedback as the executable signal, and search for test suites that are both small and strong with a quality-diversity evolutionary algorithm, so that the signal is dense and informative. TeSearch treats suite size as a behavior descriptor, so that quality is pursued at every size and a small, strong suite is reachable, and learns a transition kernel during the search that denoises the LLM as an actuator on that descriptor. Across 129 real-world modules and three backbones, TeSearch delivers the highest-quality suites of any method compared, LLM-driven evolutionary search included, and the smallest of any LLM-based one. Used as the improvement signal of a self-evolving agent, it rejects 59.9% of behavior-changing rewrites against 25.3% for the repository's tests, at a 4.0% false-alarm rate and 3.5 s per decision. The code and data are available at https://github.com/ccsnow127/TeSearch."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15708", "CorpusId": 292894063}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/524115a5b4c3cc9a81c0750a036980abfbe8fff6", "title": "SpecForge: Recursive Self-Improvement for Coding Agents via Behavioral Specifications", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15708, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328160293", "name": "Yu-Tong Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2204622281", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2304180290", "name": "Peng Gao"}, {"authorId": "2283249309", "name": "Wei Cheng"}], "abstract": "Coding agents based on frozen language models can improve their performance by refining the external context they rely on, without updating model weights. We study recursive self-improvement through behavioral specifications: natural-language code documentation that describes the behavior of program entities and guides agents in generating correct code. We formulate a self-improvement loop in which an agent generates code from its current specifications, evaluates it with execution-verified tests, and refines the specifications based on observed failures. The improved specifications then guide subsequent generation, progressively improving the agent's understanding of the codebase. However, recursive refinement is challenging because program entities are interdependent. Improving the specification of one entity can inadvertently degrade the correctness of others, a phenomenon we call output coupling. This creates a whack-a-mole effect in which local improvements cause regressions elsewhere, preventing consistent progress. We introduce SpecForge, a multi-frontier tree search framework that addresses this challenge with three mechanisms: (i) Pareto-guided state selection preserves complementary specification states and prevents premature commitment; (ii) dependency-constrained entity selection prioritizes callee-before-caller refinement; and (iii) error-diversified expansion explores multiple refinements using different subsets of test failures. On DevEval+, SpecForge achieves the best performance across five backbone models, including a 94.9% solve rate with Claude-4.5-Sonnet and a 5.7-percentage-point average improvement over the strongest baseline. The improved specifications also make the agent better at tasks outside the loop: they transfer to cross-language translation and to new-feature implementation, where they outperform the benchmark-provided requirement documents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2a4e1394b92df0d1e89de3e5c2f60269826da244", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15492", "CorpusId": 292894944}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2a4e1394b92df0d1e89de3e5c2f60269826da244", "title": "When Skills Remain But Access Fails: Factorized Skill Memory for Continual Skill Internalization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15492, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364870358", "name": "Hao-Miao Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2510634213", "name": "Yi-Ning Tang"}, {"authorId": "2510517174", "name": "Hong-Yi Wan"}, {"authorId": "2118133863", "name": "Chang-Feng Sun"}, {"authorId": "2363807483", "name": "Zi-Yue Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2319850720", "name": "Miao Zhang"}], "abstract": "Skill internalization aims to convert externally specified procedural skills into persistent model capabilities, allowing language-model agents to execute them without retrieving or reinjecting skill descriptions at inference time. Yet when new skills are learned continually, whether previously internalized skills remain usable is still poorly understood. We study forgetting in continual skill internalization and uncover a clear hierarchical structure. Early in subsequent training, a model may lose the ability to invoke an old skill from the raw request, while supplying only the correct canonical skill name substantially restores the learned behavior; with further optimization, this conditional execution also degrades. We call these two stages skill-addressing forgetting and skill-execution forgetting. Guided by this finding, we introduce Factorized Skill Memory (FSM), which separates continual skill memory into a plastic Address Memory and a stable Execution Memory. At each stage, FSM trains a new LoRA executor using only current-stage data, freezes it after acquisition, and appends it to Execution Memory; in parallel, Address Memory incrementally learns to map current requests to executors without replaying historical requests or execution trajectories. At inference time, Address Memory selects one executor from the raw request, and only that frozen executor performs a single language-model generation. On a five-stage benchmark built from 100 TaskBench skills, FSM achieves 65.95% mean exact match and 93.38% addressing accuracy across two task orders, improving over the strongest replay-free parameter-isolation baseline by 14.3 percentage points while remaining within 1.9 points of Replay, which retains historical training examples."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9f0d90c0e3fa20e4caf2a9959a2d75e0be742ffc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15969", "CorpusId": 292895432}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9f0d90c0e3fa20e4caf2a9959a2d75e0be742ffc", "title": "PopGen: Generating Semantically Diverse Populations for Zero-Shot Coordination", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15969, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2322796738", "name": "Paul Teiletche"}, {"authorId": "2290490710", "name": "Kanghyun Ryu"}, {"authorId": "2507854459", "name": "K. Park"}, {"authorId": "7794353", "name": "Dhruva Tirumala"}, {"authorId": "2271092152", "name": "Markus Wulfmeier"}, {"authorId": "144703985", "name": "Negar Mehr"}], "abstract": "In multi-agent reinforcement learning, exposing a cooperator to partners with diverse strategies during training is key to better collaboration at test time. However, existing methods typically rely on trajectory-level proxies to measure diversity, which may label two distinct trajectories of the same strategy as diverse. Instead, our key insight is to leverage LLMs'ability to describe behaviorally distinct agents in environments, similar to how humans can recognize high-level strategies. To this end, we introduce PopGen, a framework that synthesizes semantically diverse agents from scratch using LLMs. PopGen first generates a diverse set of textual descriptions of agent behaviors, then translates each behavior into a code policy that can be executed within the environment. We show that cooperators trained against PopGen populations achieve robust zero-shot cooperation with unseen partners, outperforming population-play baselines across four collaborative environments. We further show that semantic diversity scales with PopGen population size, improving the cooperator's worst-case performance, and we identify design choices that preserve population quality while maximizing diversity. Our code is available at https://github.com/labicon/PopGen."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b71fce0e9dd0ad8ac547be470e52f06e6d092560", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14737", "CorpusId": 292859083}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b71fce0e9dd0ad8ac547be470e52f06e6d092560", "title": "PTGPO: Prefix Tree Group Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14737, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2376388561", "name": "Ruo-Lin Chen"}, {"authorId": "2410988780", "name": "Yang Li"}, {"authorId": "2275531576", "name": "Ji-Hang Wang"}, {"authorId": "4406794", "name": "Zhuo-Ya Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2353315242", "name": "Yu-Chen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2320475967", "name": "Jia-Ming Xu"}, {"authorId": "2145947498", "name": "Qian Zhang"}], "abstract": "Training language agents on long-horizon interactive tasks is challenging because sparse terminal rewards provide weak credit signals for intermediate actions. Existing group-based methods either assign a shared trajectory-level advantage or estimate step-level advantages from state groups, which may conflate the current action with subsequent decisions. We propose Prefix Tree Group Policy Optimization (PTGPO), a critic-free method that constructs prefix trees over trajectory suffixes within anchor-state groups. PTGPO assigns each tree edge a credit given by the difference between its child and parent empirical mean returns, capturing the conditional return change revealed by each additional action. These prefix-tree credits provide fine-grained learning signals without requiring a critic or additional rollouts. Experiments on interactive benchmarks ALFWorld, WebShop and Sokoban show that PTGPO consistently improves over RL baselines, demonstrating its effectiveness."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e1a44c95c1370137eff8247b8de8f798030fb48e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14515", "CorpusId": 292859004}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e1a44c95c1370137eff8247b8de8f798030fb48e", "title": "SEPM: Self-Evolving Procedural Memory Grounded in Task Graphs for Multi-Agent Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14515, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2316205671", "name": "Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2506248409", "name": "Jun-Wei Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2385501405", "name": "Li Sun"}, {"authorId": "2510512791", "name": "Jian-Song Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2150496984", "name": "Sen Su"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon multi-agent collaboration requires memory that preserves both successful actions and the coordination structure underlying them. Existing memory systems support information sharing or procedural reuse, but often leave the evolution of shared procedures disconnected from the task dependencies and execution states of collaborating agents. This coupling also limits the direct transfer of procedural memory designed for individual agents to team settings. We propose Self-Evolving Procedural Memory (SEPM), a framework that uses task dependency graphs as a common representation for online coordination and procedural memory evolution. During execution, a lightweight shared blackboard records structured evidence alongside task dependencies, supporting the detection of goal, plan, and world-state divergence and evidence-based realignment. After execution, verified actions and dependencies are distilled into reusable procedures, while recurring failures inform warnings and constraints. The shared procedure pool evolves through creation, refinement, specialization, and retirement, guided by success and cost utility and constrained by evidence and safety requirements. Together, these mechanisms preserve the collaborative structure and applicability conditions needed to reuse experience across tasks. Evaluations on ALFWorld, WebArena, and OfficeBench under AutoGen and DyLAN show that SEPM achieves the highest task scores among the compared methods in all six benchmark settings. Under AutoGen, it improves task scores over the no-added-memory baseline by 29.85, 25.86, and 13.00 percentage points, respectively. The codebase is available at https://github.com/cookieApril/SEPM."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "99e083b8e12d0c25a0050a53a63dbdea394bccb7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14477", "CorpusId": 292859193}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/99e083b8e12d0c25a0050a53a63dbdea394bccb7", "title": "What Counts as the Same Task? Executable Contracts for Robustness Evaluation of Tool-Using Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14477, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "35324385", "name": "Shaikh Akib Shahriyar"}, {"authorId": "2090728097", "name": "Md. Akil Raihan Iftee"}, {"authorId": "37955463", "name": "Armon Barton"}, {"authorId": "2404665302", "name": "Matthew Wright"}], "abstract": "Robustness tests for tool-using agents often change the agent's environment, for example by hiding a hostile note in an invoice, and then judge the agent by what it does. The result tells us about the original task only if the changed test case is still a valid test of that task. Many evaluations decide this with weak rules, such as accepting any well-formed edit or any edit that stays close to the original in embedding space. We propose executable task contracts. A contract states which facts define a task, which edits are allowed, and what finishing the task means, and it checks each changed test case before the agent runs. We compare validity rules by scoring the same 14,336 stored agent runs under each rule. Across four encoders, embedding thresholds allowed 75-100% of edits that changed the task, such as swapping the payee, yet rejected 24-45% of valid hostile notes. With a MiniLM cosine threshold, Mistral appeared vulnerable on all 64 tasks, but only 26 had a successful valid attack. Replaying a saved solution and allowing edits to benchmark fields fail in similar ways. Evaluations should state, and check, what counts as the same task."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0ceaecccb3ad08f52828dac5ef4c68bc6f705162", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14601", "CorpusId": 292859015}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0ceaecccb3ad08f52828dac5ef4c68bc6f705162", "title": "StateSkill-Agent: An Effective and Efficient Embodied Agent via State-conditioned Skill", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14601, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2365334804", "name": "Chang-Xu Wei"}, {"authorId": "2276743977", "name": "Jing-Yi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2509646418", "name": "Zhi-Yu Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2359716309", "name": "Tian-Yi Lin"}, {"authorId": "2360844262", "name": "Yi-Xian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2190750836", "name": "Yun-Peng Qing"}, {"authorId": "2448653304", "name": "Jing-Yuan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2327007688", "name": "Zhi Hou"}, {"authorId": "2333251144", "name": "Jia-Xing Huang"}], "abstract": "In this work, we aim to develop an embodied MLLM agent that understands and solves embodied tasks by learning, abstracting, and reusing transferrable and generalizable behavioral patterns. To this end, we propose StateSkill, a new skill representation for embodied agents, which introduces the concept of\"finite-state machines\"into skill modeling for effective and efficient skill construction and learning. The core idea of StateSkill is to model embodied interaction experiences as a finite-state machine, where a skill is organized around a set of task-progress states, state transitions, and state-conditioned guidance, enabling embodied agents to dynamically adjust their skills as the task progresses while keeping skill contexts compact and stage-relevant. We further develop StateSkill-Evolve, an experience-driven evolution mechanism that iteratively refines StateSkill through retrospective analysis of new successful and failed state-guided trajectories. Upon this, we introduce StateGRPO, which integrates StateSkill evolution and model optimization via state-conditioned GRPO into a unified iterative learning loop, enabling state-conditioned skills and model capabilities to co-evolve and reinforce each other. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed methods and trained model (i.e., StateSkill-Agent) across various embodied tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "26ba0ada46f8be0431ea77eba962bc5e391bea18", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14574", "CorpusId": 292859229}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/26ba0ada46f8be0431ea77eba962bc5e391bea18", "title": "CB-Agent: Concept-Based In-Context Learning for Sequential Action Selection", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14574, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2344196874", "name": "Qing-Yue Zhang"}, {"authorId": "120710335", "name": "Hua-Ze Tang"}, {"authorId": "2491695181", "name": "Min-Yu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2510506773", "name": "Yu-Xiang Yan"}, {"authorId": "2362467369", "name": "Wen-Bo Ding"}, {"authorId": "2382487394", "name": "Shao-Lun Huang"}], "abstract": "In-context learning (ICL) allows large language models to use demonstrations without updating their parameters. For interactive agents, demonstrations are trajectories of reasoning, actions, and observations that illustrate how to complete a task. Reusing demonstrations in new situations is challenging: directly inputting full trajectories consumes limited prompt space and constrains the overall effectiveness, while natural-language summaries require the agent to translate general guidance into specific action choices. Therefore, we study whether expert action choices can directly supervise a reusable scoring rule. We propose CB-Agent, which extends concept-based ICL to sequential action selection. We extract environment-action decisions from expert trajectories and fit a concept vector by maximum likelihood to model the conditional distribution over candidate actions given the context. This model combines the concept with frozen language-model features and priors. During ReAct interaction, the agent uses this distribution to select environment actions without including demonstrations in the action-scoring context. Across TextWorld, ALFWorld, and ScienceWorld, CB-Agent achieves the highest cross-benchmark average among the evaluated methods at all three tested model sizes and ranks first on TextWorld and ScienceWorld at each size. ALFWorld experiments support the learned concept's contribution beyond prior-only scoring and show further gains when combined with Reflexion. Code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CB-Agent-DFF9/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fe167c348d2f3dbeb73c417c3f0e791613659477", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14625", "CorpusId": 292859308}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fe167c348d2f3dbeb73c417c3f0e791613659477", "title": "From Existing Methods to New Research Ideas: Two Perspectives on Recursive Self-Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14625, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455560892", "name": "Lei-Jun Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2363563602", "name": "Yan-Ke Yu"}, {"authorId": "2448359940", "name": "Zhi-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510256750", "name": "Qu Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2322618807", "name": "Tian-Cheng Hu"}, {"authorId": "2499651914", "name": "Yi-Rui Dong"}, {"authorId": "2510267448", "name": "Yi-Han Wu"}, {"authorId": "2503572515", "name": "Yang-Xi Xiang"}, {"authorId": "2455574254", "name": "Xue-Jun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2454430758", "name": "Xi Chen"}, {"authorId": "39835284", "name": "B. Qian"}, {"authorId": "2341817744", "name": "Yao-Wei Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2265725059", "name": "Jun-Hao Hu"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) offers a route to AI systems that participate in their own continued development. Recent methods allow language agents to retain changes to their parameters, memory, skills, and code for subsequent execution and improvement. We examine how these methods can inform new research ideas through two complementary perspectives. First, we introduce a unified taxonomy with nine design dimensions organized into four groups: update targets, update organization, update schedule and scope, feedback and selection. This taxonomy places existing methods in a shared design space, where examining individual dimensions and their interactions can inspire new research ideas. Second, we examine how existing RSI methods draw on traditional deep learning, for example through optimization, regularization, data augmentation, continual learning, and adversarial learning. We develop further ideas by examining more specific learning mechanisms and drawing on evolutionary computation. We also review how existing benchmarks assess self-improvement under online, offline, and offline-to-online settings. Together, these perspectives help researchers develop new RSI research ideas from existing designs and learning mechanisms. RSI papers and resources are available at https://prism-shadow.github.io/awesome-rsi/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dce4deeae7b56fe7dca0d3fa4ba76b890a6329f8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14977", "CorpusId": 292895073}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dce4deeae7b56fe7dca0d3fa4ba76b890a6329f8", "title": "EnactOpt: Diagnosis-Guided Harness Evolution for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14977, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2406050612", "name": "Zhi-Yuan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2352886718", "name": "Zhuo-Shi Pan"}, {"authorId": "2392723980", "name": "Zhao-Lu Kang"}, {"authorId": "2510533300", "name": "Zhong-Xia Chen"}, {"authorId": "2402763425", "name": "Shuo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2510556629", "name": "S. Ye"}, {"authorId": "2510662756", "name": "Heng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506266474", "name": "Han-Yu Li"}], "abstract": "Embodied agents built on foundation models rely on an execution harness to organize observations, guide actions, and incorporate execution feedback into subsequent decisions. Failures can arise not only from limited model capabilities, but also from how the harness presents evidence and guides its use. These model-external mechanisms provide an adaptation target without updating model parameters, but task-specific interaction experience can be costly to collect. Task-level outcomes alone do not reveal whether relevant evidence was missing from the model input or was available but inadequately used. Moreover, these two aspects are coupled: effective action guidance may require evidence that the current context omits, while exposing that evidence alone does not ensure an appropriate response. We introduce EnactOpt, which uses limited execution feedback to jointly evolve reusable textual skills and context-construction code while keeping the underlying models and runtime fixed. It diagnoses adjacent decisions by relating action feedback to the actual model input and subsequent action. Structured episode summaries guide joint revisions through prompt-level edit operators, and candidate harnesses undergo compatibility checks and validation-based retention. Across VLABench, ALFWorld, and Embodied Agent Interface (EAI), EnactOpt improves task success by approximately 10-27 percentage points over the initial harnesses and outperforms the evaluated GEPA, TextGrad, and Trace2Skill baselines. These gains extend to task conditions not used for harness evolution or candidate selection. The results support a complementary route to embodied adaptation: turning limited execution experience into reusable behavioral improvements by jointly refining how evidence is presented and acted upon."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b80d24feb56b468ec9ce8f1b282ed95de01cecb6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14966", "CorpusId": 292894426}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b80d24feb56b468ec9ce8f1b282ed95de01cecb6", "title": "SkillHorizon: Constructing Long-Horizon Agent Tasks through Skill Composition", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14966, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2454472044", "name": "Yuxuan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2391115799", "name": "Zhao-Chen Su"}, {"authorId": "2405115995", "name": "Hang-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2390991707", "name": "Shi-Jue Huang"}, {"authorId": "2338346463", "name": "Zhixiong Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2113992095", "name": "Ka-Po Chung"}, {"authorId": "2251530010", "name": "Zhong-Wei Xie"}, {"authorId": "2209990450", "name": "Tianshi ZHENG"}, {"authorId": "2262397115", "name": "Wei Fan"}, {"authorId": "2256995117", "name": "Qing Zong"}, {"authorId": "2257452757", "name": "Hao-Chen Shi"}, {"authorId": "2257133167", "name": "Bai-Xuan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2325943665", "name": "Hao-Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2241325169", "name": "Yang-Qiu Song"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon agents must complete workflows in which later activities depend on earlier outputs and changes to shared state. A key challenge for evaluation is to expand task requirements and their dependencies while keeping tasks executable and outcomes verifiable. We introduce SkillHorizon, a benchmark that constructs long-horizon tasks through skill composition in seven business environments. Its generators use domain-specific rules to turn reusable skill procedures into concrete business activities. These activities are connected through input-output dependencies, shared resources, and state updates, allowing workload and coordination demands to grow together. Successful reference executions validate task feasibility and measure interaction workload, while programmatic evaluators assess local completion and whole-task success. We evaluate five language models on 1,050 tasks with reference interaction workloads ranging from 64K to 1M tokens. Across these scales, mean local-goal completion falls from 90.4% to 46.8%, as completed work fails to keep pace with growing requirements. At larger workloads, agents more often submit unsuccessful final answers with substantial work unfinished and tool-call allowance remaining. Trace analysis also identifies cases where scripts execute successfully but their outputs fail downstream business checks. SkillHorizon provides a configurable testbed for studying sustained progress, dependency coordination, and complete task delivery in growing workflows. Our code is available at https://github.com/KnowComp/SkillHorizon."}}]}
